    static let sections: [StudySection] = [
        // Section 1: Explain the factors that influence key Contact Center metric (12 topics)
        StudySection(
            id: "s1_explain_the_factors_that_influence_key_c",
            title: "Explain the factors that influence key Contact Center metrics and key performance indicators (...)",
            color: Color(red: 0.30, green: 0.50, blue: 0.80),
            lightColor: Color(red: 0.90, green: 0.94, blue: 1.00),
            icon: "doc.text.fill",
            objectives: [
                StudyObjective(
                    id: "s1_explain_the_factors_that_influence_key_c_obj",
                    title: "Explain the factors that influence key Contact Center metrics and key performance indicators (...)",
                    topics: [
                        StudyTopic(id: "s1_explain_the_factors_that_influence_key_c_1", number: 1, question: "The difference between synchronous contact center models (phone, live chat) and asynchronous models (email, messaging)", answer: "Synchronous channels require both the agent and the customer to be present at the same time (phone calls, live chat). The interaction is real-time; if either party is unavailable, the session ends or the customer waits in a queue.\n\nAsynchronous channels allow participants to respond on their own schedule (email, SMS/messaging). A customer sends a message and can do other things while waiting for a reply. Agents can handle more concurrent interactions because they are not locked into a continuous real-time session.\n\nKey exam implication: synchronous channels typically produce lower AHT per interaction but require dedicated agent availability. Asynchronous channels improve agent utilization but can extend total resolution time."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s1_explain_the_factors_that_influence_key_c_2", number: 2, question: "How Average Handle Time (AHT) is calculated in a contact center", answer: "AHT = (Talk Time + Hold Time + After-Call Work) / Total Interactions\n\n- Talk Time: actual time spent communicating with the customer.\n\n- Hold Time: time the customer is placed on hold during the interaction.\n\n- After-Call Work (ACW): time the agent spends on tasks after the interaction ends (logging, case updates, etc.).\n\nFor non-voice channels, substitute \"talk time\" with \"active handle time.\" AHT is an average across all interactions in the measured period."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s1_explain_the_factors_that_influence_key_c_3", number: 3, question: "Which operational factors — agent skill mix, knowledge availability, and escalation rate — most directly influence AHT", answer: "- Agent skill mix: agents lacking product or process knowledge take longer to resolve issues, raising AHT. Better training and skill-based routing reduce this.\n\n- Knowledge availability: agents who can quickly find accurate articles resolve issues faster. Poor knowledge base coverage forces agents to search longer or escalate.\n\n- Escalation rate: every escalation adds handle time to the original interaction and creates a new interaction at a higher tier. High escalation rates inflate overall AHT across the center.\n\nThe exam often tests the ability to identify which lever to pull first. If AHT is high due to escalations, fix routing or knowledge — not just headcount."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s1_explain_the_factors_that_influence_key_c_4", number: 4, question: "How Customer Satisfaction (CSAT) and Net Promoter Score (NPS) differ as KPIs for contact center performance", answer: "| | CSAT | NPS |\n\n|---|---|---|\n\n| What it measures | Satisfaction with a specific interaction | Overall loyalty / likelihood to recommend |\n\n| Timing | Immediately post-interaction | Periodically (quarterly, annually) |\n\n| Scale | Typically 1–5 or 1–10 per transaction | 0–10; grouped into Promoters, Passives, Detractors |\n\n| Scope | Transactional | Relational |\n\nCSAT reflects quality of a single support experience. NPS reflects the cumulative brand relationship, which support is only one component of."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s1_explain_the_factors_that_influence_key_c_5", number: 5, question: "When CSAT versus NPS is the more appropriate metric to report on contact center performance", answer: "- Use CSAT when you need to evaluate the quality of individual interactions, measure agent performance, or identify specific process failures in the contact center.\n\n- Use NPS when evaluating the overall customer relationship health, benchmarking against industry peers, or reporting to executive leadership on brand loyalty trends.\n\nContact center managers typically own CSAT. NPS is typically reported at the company/product level and owned by customer success or marketing teams."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s1_explain_the_factors_that_influence_key_c_6", number: 6, question: "How First Contact Resolution (FCR) is impacted by agent empowerment, escalation paths, and access to knowledge articles", answer: "- Agent empowerment: agents who have authority to issue refunds, make exceptions, or access account systems can resolve more issues in one interaction.\n\n- Escalation paths: complex or poorly defined escalation paths force agents to transfer cases unnecessarily, destroying FCR.\n\n- Knowledge articles: when agents can quickly find accurate, up-to-date answers, they resolve issues without needing to call back or transfer.\n\nFCR is calculated as: (Cases resolved on first contact / Total cases) x 100. It is one of the strongest predictors of CSAT."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s1_explain_the_factors_that_influence_key_c_7", number: 7, question: "How abandon rate and queue wait time are related in a contact center", answer: "Abandon rate is the percentage of customers who disconnect before reaching an agent. It has a direct positive correlation with queue wait time: as wait times increase, more customers abandon.\n\nA common industry threshold is that abandon rate spikes significantly once wait time exceeds 2 minutes for phone support. Reducing wait time through better staffing, self-service deflection, or callback options directly reduces abandon rate."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s1_explain_the_factors_that_influence_key_c_8", number: 8, question: "Which configuration or staffing changes most directly reduce customer abandonment", answer: "- Staffing: add agents during peak hours (workforce management/forecasting).\n\n- Virtual hold / callback: offer customers a callback instead of waiting, eliminating the cost of holding.\n\n- Self-service deflection: IVR menus, chatbots, and Experience Cloud portals resolve simple issues before they reach the queue.\n\n- Omni-Channel routing optimization: balance load across available agents to reduce individual queue depth.\n\n- Business hours messaging: set customer expectations and route off-hours contacts to async channels."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s1_explain_the_factors_that_influence_key_c_9", number: 9, question: "What drives escalation rates in a contact center and how routing configuration, agent training gaps, and self-service availability contribute", answer: "- Routing misconfiguration: cases routed to agents without the right skills force escalation to a more qualified tier.\n\n- Agent training gaps: agents unsure how to handle certain products or issue types escalate rather than resolve.\n\n- Insufficient self-service: customers contact agents for issues the portal or chatbot could have resolved, clogging queues with simple requests and pushing complex issues toward escalation.\n\nTo reduce escalation rate: implement skills-based routing, invest in knowledge and training, and deploy self-service for tier-1 issue types."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s1_explain_the_factors_that_influence_key_c_10", number: 10, question: "How service level agreements (SLAs) and response time KPIs interrelate", answer: "An SLA is a contractual commitment to respond to or resolve a case within a defined timeframe (e.g., \"P1 cases responded to within 1 hour\"). Response time KPIs measure actual performance against those targets.\n\nIn Salesforce, Entitlement Processes and Milestones enforce SLA commitments. SLA compliance rate (% of cases where milestones were met) is reported in Salesforce reports using the CaseMilestone object."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s1_explain_the_factors_that_influence_key_c_11", number: 11, question: "How cost-per-contact is affected by deflection strategy choices, including self-service portals, chatbots, and knowledge base accessibility", answer: "Cost-per-contact = Total contact center operating cost / Total contacts handled\n\nDeflection strategies reduce the denominator (contacts handled by agents) without reducing operating costs proportionately, or they allow cost reduction through fewer FTEs:\n\n- Self-service portal / Knowledge: customers resolve issues themselves; zero agent cost.\n\n- Chatbots / AI agents: handle tier-1 volume at a fraction of agent cost per resolution.\n\n- Knowledge base accessibility: faster agent resolution = lower AHT = lower cost-per-contact even when contacts are not deflected.\n\nThe exam expects you to connect deflection investment to cost reduction and also to recognize that poor deflection design can increase CSAT issues."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s1_explain_the_factors_that_influence_key_c_12", number: 12, question: "What happens to KPI reporting when business hours are not configured correctly", answer: "- Milestone timers continue to run 24/7 instead of pausing outside business hours, causing false SLA breaches in reports.\n\n- Escalation rules may trigger prematurely because time-based criteria do not account for non-business hours.\n\n- Response time metrics are inflated when business hours are not factored into calculations, making performance look worse than it is.\n\n- Entitlement process accuracy degrades, making SLA compliance data unreliable for executive reporting.\n\nAlways configure Business Hours in Setup and associate them with Entitlement Processes and Escalation Rules.")
                    ]
                )
            ]
        ),

        // Section 2: Determine the risks, benefits, and business challenges of me (7 topics)
        StudySection(
            id: "s2_determine_the_risks_benefits_and_busines",
            title: "Determine the risks, benefits, and business challenges of meeting a client's desired outcome.",
            color: Color(red: 0.20, green: 0.60, blue: 0.50),
            lightColor: Color(red: 0.88, green: 0.96, blue: 0.94),
            icon: "checkmark.shield.fill",
            objectives: [
                StudyObjective(
                    id: "s2_determine_the_risks_benefits_and_busines_obj",
                    title: "Determine the risks, benefits, and business challenges of meeting a client's desired outcome.",
                    topics: [
                        StudyTopic(id: "s2_determine_the_risks_benefits_and_busines_1", number: 1, question: "How to assess the risk of consolidating multiple legacy support channels into a single omni-channel Salesforce implementation for a large enterprise", answer: "Key risks to assess:\n\n- Data migration complexity: each legacy channel likely has different data models. Mapping and cleansing is time-consuming.\n\n- Integration dependencies: legacy channels may feed downstream systems (billing, ERP) that need to be re-pointed.\n\n- Agent change resistance: agents trained on legacy tools resist new workflows.\n\n- Channel parity gaps: not all legacy channel features map 1:1 to Salesforce; feature gaps can delay go-live.\n\n- Single point of failure: consolidation increases the blast radius of any outage.\n\nMitigation: phased rollout by channel, parallel run periods, thorough gap analysis before design begins."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s2_determine_the_risks_benefits_and_busines_2", number: 2, question: "What organizational change management risks arise when migrating an on-premise contact center to Service Cloud, and how a consultant should address adoption resistance", answer: "- Resistance from agents: fear of monitoring, job loss, or steep learning curves.\n\n- Middle management skepticism: supervisors may feel new dashboards expose their team's performance gaps.\n\n- IT resistance: on-premise teams may resist cloud dependency and loss of infrastructure control.\n\nConsultant responses:\n\n- Engage executive sponsors early to communicate strategic vision.\n\n- Involve agent representatives in UAT and design workshops (\"champions\").\n\n- Provide role-based training before go-live.\n\n- Use a hypercare period with on-site support to build confidence."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s2_determine_the_risks_benefits_and_busines_3", number: 3, question: "How to identify and quantify the business value of a Knowledge Management investment in a contact center setting using measurable KPIs", answer: "Business value metrics for Knowledge:\n\n- AHT reduction: track AHT before and after knowledge deployment.\n\n- FCR improvement: measure rate of single-interaction resolutions.\n\n- Case deflection rate: count self-service searches that resulted in no case creation.\n\n- Article attach rate: % of cases where an agent attached a Knowledge article.\n\n- Cost-per-contact reduction: calculate savings from AHT and deflection improvements.\n\nPresent these as a before/after comparison tied to dollar value (e.g., 15 seconds AHT reduction x 10,000 contacts/month = X agent hours saved per month)."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s2_determine_the_risks_benefits_and_busines_4", number: 4, question: "The business challenges involved when a client wants to reduce AHT by 30% without impacting CSAT, and which solution trade-offs are most relevant", answer: "A 30% AHT reduction without CSAT impact is achievable but requires careful trade-off analysis:\n\n- Knowledge optimization reduces handle time without cutting quality (good trade-off).\n\n- Macro / scripted responses speed resolution but can feel impersonal if overused (CSAT risk).\n\n- Hard time limits on agents reduce AHT but may force premature case closure, driving repeat contacts and hurting FCR and CSAT.\n\n- Automation for simple cases: deflect or auto-resolve low-complexity cases; agents handle harder ones without rushing.\n\nThe consultant should recommend: improved knowledge, macros, screen pop automation, and agent tools — not arbitrary time caps."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s2_determine_the_risks_benefits_and_busines_5", number: 5, question: "Given a client requesting 24/7 live agent coverage, which risks and costs must be weighed against the benefits compared to an AI-assisted self-service approach", answer: "Live agent 24/7 risks/costs:\n\n- Overnight and weekend shifts require additional FTEs or outsourcing at premium cost.\n\n- Staffing thin overnight shifts leads to longer wait times and lower quality.\n\n- High cost-per-contact during off-peak hours.\n\nAI-assisted self-service benefits:\n\n- Available 24/7 at a fraction of the cost.\n\n- Consistent responses for common queries.\n\n- Escalates to live agent during business hours if unresolved.\n\nKey consideration: what is the nature of after-hours contacts? If they are mostly simple (password resets, order status), AI deflection handles them well. Complex issues or regulated industries (healthcare, finance) may require live agent coverage or escalation queues."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s2_determine_the_risks_benefits_and_busines_6", number: 6, question: "When a client wants to implement an AI agent to handle tier-1 support, what risks around trust, compliance, and escalation failure must be evaluated before recommending deployment", answer: "- Trust: customers must be informed they are interacting with an AI (transparency, especially in regulated industries).\n\n- Compliance: AI agents must not handle PII in ways that violate GDPR, CCPA, or HIPAA. Actions taken by AI (e.g., refunds, cancellations) must have appropriate guardrails.\n\n- Escalation failure: if the AI cannot recognize the need to escalate, customers may be stuck in loops, destroying CSAT.\n\n- Hallucination / incorrect information: AI agents must be grounded in verified knowledge sources, not free-form generation.\n\n- Audit trail: all AI agent actions should be logged for compliance and quality review.\n\nRecommendation: start with low-risk, high-volume, well-defined use cases; implement clear escalation triggers; test extensively before broad deployment."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s2_determine_the_risks_benefits_and_busines_7", number: 7, question: "How over-automation of case routing and closure can negatively affect CSAT and what business safeguards should be included when recommending automation-heavy designs", answer: "Over-automation risks:\n\n- Auto-closure of inactive cases: customers perceive their issue as ignored if a case is closed without resolution confirmation.\n\n- Routing to wrong queues: over-reliance on keyword-based routing misclassifies cases.\n\n- Auto-responses that feel robotic: high volume of automated emails frustrates customers.\n\nBusiness safeguards:\n\n- Require customer confirmation before auto-closing cases (\"Does this solve your issue?\").\n\n- Include human review queues for cases flagged by automation as resolved.\n\n- Monitor CSAT scores specifically for auto-closed cases.\n\n- Allow agents to override automated routing decisions.")
                    ]
                )
            ]
        ),

