        // Section 4: Given a scenario, recommend appropriate Service Cloud deploy (13 topics)
        StudySection(
            id: "s4_given_a_scenario_recommend_appropriate_s",
            title: "Given a scenario, recommend appropriate Service Cloud deployment and training strategies.",
            color: Color(red: 0.80, green: 0.40, blue: 0.20),
            lightColor: Color(red: 1.00, green: 0.94, blue: 0.88),
            icon: "chart.bar.xaxis.ascending",
            objectives: [
                StudyObjective(
                    id: "s4_given_a_scenario_recommend_appropriate_s_obj",
                    title: "Given a scenario, recommend appropriate Service Cloud deployment and training strategies.",
                    topics: [
                        StudyTopic(id: "s4_given_a_scenario_recommend_appropriate_s_1", number: 1, question: "What sandbox strategy (Developer, Partial Copy, Full Copy) is appropriate for different stages of a Service Cloud implementation, and why", answer: "| Sandbox Type | Best For | Why |\n\n|---|---|---|\n\n| Developer | Initial build, unit testing | Free, fast refresh, no production data needed |\n\n| Partial Copy | System/integration testing | Subset of production data for realistic testing without full volume |\n\n| Full Copy | UAT, performance testing, cutover rehearsal | Mirrors production exactly; validates data migration and performance at scale |\n\nKey rule: never use production for development or testing. Use Developer sandboxes early and promote to Full Copy for UAT."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s4_given_a_scenario_recommend_appropriate_s_2", number: 2, question: "When a company has 500 agents across 3 regions with different business processes, how to determine whether a phased rollout is preferable to a big-bang deployment", answer: "Phased rollout is preferable when:\n\n- Business processes vary significantly by region (different routing rules, queues, SLAs).\n\n- Risk tolerance is low — one region going live validates the approach before broader rollout.\n\n- Training resources are limited and cannot support all 500 agents simultaneously.\n\n- Data migration for each region is independent.\n\nBig-bang may work when:\n\n- Processes are largely uniform across regions.\n\n- A hard cutover deadline is contractually required.\n\n- Integration systems cannot support partial rollout.\n\nFor 3 regions with different processes, a phased rollout by region is almost always the safer recommendation."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s4_given_a_scenario_recommend_appropriate_s_3", number: 3, question: "When to use a pilot group for phased deployment versus deploying to all users simultaneously, and how this decision depends on risk tolerance and data migration readiness", answer: "Use a pilot group when:\n\n- Risk tolerance is low.\n\n- Processes are complex or untested in production-like conditions.\n\n- Data migration has not been fully validated at scale.\n\n- A small group can provide feedback that improves the experience for the broader rollout.\n\nDeploy to all users simultaneously when:\n\n- Risk tolerance is high and business demands a clean cutover.\n\n- The pilot would create confusion (e.g., two parallel systems for the same process).\n\n- Data migration has been fully validated and the org is stable.\n\nPilots typically include 5–10% of users, chosen to represent diverse roles and use cases."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s4_given_a_scenario_recommend_appropriate_s_4", number: 4, question: "How to select between train-the-trainer, role-based, and self-paced training strategies based on agent role diversity and go-live timeline", answer: "| Strategy | Best When |\n\n|---|---|\n\n| Train-the-Trainer | Large agent population, geographically distributed, tight budget; master trainers replicate training locally |\n\n| Role-Based | Multiple distinct agent roles with significantly different workflows; ensures each group gets relevant content |\n\n| Self-Paced (Trailhead/LMS) | Spread-out go-live timeline; agents can train at their own speed; good as supplemental training |\n\nFor 500+ agents across 3 regions with diverse roles, combine: train-the-trainer for scale + role-based content modules to ensure relevance."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s4_given_a_scenario_recommend_appropriate_s_5", number: 5, question: "How to plan a data freeze window and cutover sequence when migrating from a legacy case management system to Service Cloud with minimal disruption to agent operations", answer: "Data freeze window: a period before cutover when no new data is entered into the legacy system (or changes are logged separately). This allows the final migration to be current.\n\nCutover sequence:"),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s4_given_a_scenario_recommend_appropriate_s_6", number: 6, question: "Complete and validate final full data migration in a Full Copy sandbox.", answer: ""),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s4_given_a_scenario_recommend_appropriate_s_7", number: 7, question: "Communicate data freeze to all users (typically 24–48 hours before go-live).", answer: ""),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s4_given_a_scenario_recommend_appropriate_s_8", number: 8, question: "Run final delta migration of records created since last full migration.", answer: ""),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s4_given_a_scenario_recommend_appropriate_s_9", number: 9, question: "Validate migrated data in production (record counts, spot checks, relationship integrity).", answer: ""),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s4_given_a_scenario_recommend_appropriate_s_10", number: 10, question: "Flip DNS / routing to new system.", answer: ""),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s4_given_a_scenario_recommend_appropriate_s_11", number: 11, question: "Confirm agents can log in and work cases.", answer: ""),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s4_given_a_scenario_recommend_appropriate_s_12", number: 12, question: "Decommission legacy system access (or set to read-only).", answer: "Rehearse the cutover sequence at least once in a Full Copy sandbox before the real cutover."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s4_given_a_scenario_recommend_appropriate_s_13", number: 13, question: "How to design a hypercare plan for the weeks immediately following a Service Cloud go-live event", answer: "A hypercare plan includes:\n\n- Dedicated support team: project team members (admin, developer, consultant) on standby for rapid issue resolution.\n\n- Escalation paths: clear tiers from agent issue to admin to developer to vendor support.\n\n- Issue log: shared tracker for all post-go-live bugs, questions, and enhancement requests.\n\n- Daily stand-ups: brief check-ins with the client to review the issue log and resolve blockers.\n\n- Agent office hours: sessions where agents can ask questions in real time.\n\n- Duration: typically 2–4 weeks, with formal exit criteria (e.g., issue log below a defined threshold, no P1 bugs open).")
                    ]
                )
            ]
        ),

        // Section 5: Explain the considerations and implications for data migrati (11 topics)
        StudySection(
            id: "s5_explain_the_considerations_and_implicati",
            title: "Explain the considerations and implications for data migration, data quality, data governance, and large data volumes.",
            color: Color(red: 0.25, green: 0.55, blue: 0.70),
            lightColor: Color(red: 0.88, green: 0.95, blue: 0.98),
            icon: "globe",
            objectives: [
                StudyObjective(
                    id: "s5_explain_the_considerations_and_implicati_obj",
                    title: "Explain the considerations and implications for data migration, data quality, data governance, and large data volumes.",
                    topics: [
                        StudyTopic(id: "s5_explain_the_considerations_and_implicati_1", number: 1, question: "What data quality issues commonly arise during a Service Cloud migration and how to address duplicate records, missing owner lookups, and incomplete case histories", answer: "Common issues:\n\n- Duplicate contacts/accounts: use Salesforce duplicate rules and matching rules, or pre-clean with deduplication tools (e.g., Dedupe Solutions, RingLead).\n\n- Missing owner lookups: legacy system user IDs do not map to Salesforce users. Solution: create a crosswalk file mapping legacy user IDs to Salesforce User IDs before migration.\n\n- Incomplete case histories: legacy systems may not have full activity logs. Set expectations with the client early: some history may need to be migrated as attachments or flat notes rather than structured records.\n\nAlways run a data profiling exercise before migration planning to quantify the scope of quality issues."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s5_explain_the_considerations_and_implicati_2", number: 2, question: "How to design a data migration strategy for moving historical case records from a legacy CRM to Salesforce, including handling of related records and attachments", answer: "Migration sequence matters — parent records must exist before children:"),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s5_explain_the_considerations_and_implicati_3", number: 3, question: "**Accounts** (parent of Contact and Case).", answer: "2. Contacts."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s5_explain_the_considerations_and_implicati_4", number: 4, question: "**Cases** (reference Account and Contact).", answer: ""),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s5_explain_the_considerations_and_implicati_5", number: 5, question: "**Case Comments** (child of Case).", answer: ""),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s5_explain_the_considerations_and_implicati_6", number: 6, question: "**Attachments / Files** (child of Case; use ContentVersion for Files).", answer: ""),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s5_explain_the_considerations_and_implicati_7", number: 7, question: "**Email Messages** (child of Case).", answer: "Design considerations:\n\n- Map legacy fields to Salesforce fields (field mapping document).\n\n- Decide what historical data to migrate vs. archive (e.g., cases older than 3 years may go to a data archive).\n\n- Validate counts at each step.\n\n- Perform a parallel run where possible before cutover."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s5_explain_the_considerations_and_implicati_8", number: 8, question: "When to use Bulk API versus Data Loader versus third-party ETL tools for migrating large volumes of case and contact records into Salesforce", answer: "| Tool | Best For |\n\n|---|---|\n\n| Data Loader | Mid-volume migrations (up to ~5M records); simple, UI-driven; good for one-time or admin-managed loads |\n\n| Bulk API | Large volumes (millions of records); asynchronous; used programmatically or via Data Loader in Bulk API mode |\n\n| Third-party ETL (MuleSoft, Informatica, Talend) | Complex transformations, multi-system orchestration, recurring sync jobs, or when business logic must be applied during migration |\n\nBulk API is the standard choice for large-scale migrations. ETL tools add value when the migration involves complex joins, transformations, or ongoing sync needs."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s5_explain_the_considerations_and_implicati_9", number: 9, question: "How large data volumes affect Service Cloud performance", answer: "- Report performance degrades when report objects (Cases, Activities) contain tens of millions of records without proper filters.\n\n- SOQL query timeouts can occur in Apex and integrations if queries lack selective indexed filters on large objects.\n\n- Page load times slow if Related Lists on Case records contain thousands of child records (emails, activities).\n\n- Skinny tables: Salesforce creates these for orgs with large data volumes to improve query performance on frequently accessed columns.\n\n- Archiving: use Field Audit Trail or Big Objects to archive old case data and keep the primary object lean.\n\n- Indexes: work with Salesforce Support to add custom indexes on fields frequently used in search/filter criteria for large tables."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s5_explain_the_considerations_and_implicati_10", number: 10, question: "The data governance considerations for PII handling within a Service Cloud environment", answer: "- Field-Level Security (FLS): restrict PII fields (SSN, date of birth, credit card) to only profiles/permission sets that require access.\n\n- Data masking: in non-production sandboxes, mask PII to prevent exposure during testing.\n\n- Encryption: use Salesforce Shield Platform Encryption for PII fields at rest.\n\n- Retention policies: define how long PII is retained and automate deletion or anonymization using flows or external tools.\n\n- Access logging: Salesforce Shield Event Monitoring logs who accessed PII fields.\n\n- GDPR/CCPA compliance: honor data subject rights (erasure, portability) — Salesforce does not natively automate erasure; custom automation is required."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s5_explain_the_considerations_and_implicati_11", number: 11, question: "The data governance considerations for managing case data ownership and retention policies within a Service Cloud environment", answer: "- Case ownership: define who owns cases (queue vs. named agent) and how ownership transfers during agent absence or role changes. Use assignment rules to enforce ownership policy.\n\n- Retention policies: define how long closed cases are retained in Salesforce before archiving or deletion. Document this in a data governance policy.\n\n- Archiving strategy: use Big Objects or external archiving tools for cases past the retention window. Maintain read-only access for compliance.\n\n- Audit trail: Salesforce Field History Tracking (up to 20 fields per object) provides a history of changes to case fields for compliance.\n\n- Role-based access to historical cases: ensure that archived or old cases are accessible only to authorized roles (e.g., compliance, legal).")
                    ]
                )
            ]
        ),

        // Section 6: Given a scenario, analyze customer requirements to determine (9 topics)
        StudySection(
            id: "s6_given_a_scenario_analyze_customer_requir",
            title: "Given a scenario, analyze customer requirements to determine an appropriate solution design considering capabilities, limitations, and design trade-offs for the service rep experience.",
            color: Color(red: 0.70, green: 0.30, blue: 0.40),
            lightColor: Color(red: 0.98, green: 0.90, blue: 0.92),
            icon: "person.2.fill",
            objectives: [
                StudyObjective(
                    id: "s6_given_a_scenario_analyze_customer_requir_obj",
                    title: "Given a scenario, analyze customer requirements to determine an appropriate solution design considering capabilities, limitations, and design trade-offs for the service rep experience.",
                    topics: [
                        StudyTopic(id: "s6_given_a_scenario_analyze_customer_requir_1", number: 1, question: "The difference between a Lightning App and a Lightning Console App, and when a consultant should recommend a Service Console App over a standard Lightning App for agent workflows", answer: "Lightning App: standard multi-tab experience; each record opens in a new browser tab. Not optimized for agents who need to work on multiple records simultaneously.\n\nLightning Console App: multi-tab experience within a single browser tab. Agents can have multiple cases, contacts, and accounts open simultaneously in subtabs without losing context. Includes utility bar for persistent tools (phone, macros, notes).\n\nRecommend Service Console when:\n\n- Agents handle multiple concurrent interactions.\n\n- Phone/chat/messaging channels require persistent utility bar components.\n\n- Case context switching is frequent (agents need to move between records without losing position).\n\nFor back-office or field users who work one record at a time, a standard Lightning App is sufficient."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s6_given_a_scenario_analyze_customer_requir_2", number: 2, question: "How to design a Lightning Service Console layout that optimizes agent efficiency for a high-volume phone support team, including the use of utility bar, macros, and split view", answer: "Key design elements:\n\n- Utility Bar: add softphone (CTI), macros, history, and notes components. These persist across all tabs so agents never lose access to their phone tool.\n\n- Macros: automate multi-step repetitive tasks (e.g., send email, update status, add comment) with a single click. Reduce AHT significantly.\n\n- Split View: allows agents to view a list of cases on the left while working a case on the right. Ideal for agents monitoring a queue simultaneously.\n\n- Page layout: prioritize the most-used fields and related lists at the top. Use Dynamic Forms to show/hide fields based on case type or status.\n\n- Quick Text: pre-written phrases accessible during email/chat to reduce typing time."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s6_given_a_scenario_analyze_customer_requir_3", number: 3, question: "The trade-offs between a single universal agent console layout versus role-specific page layouts for a multi-tier support organization", answer: "| Approach | Pros | Cons |\n\n|---|---|---|\n\n| Single universal layout | Easier to maintain; agents can be moved between tiers without retraining | Cluttered with irrelevant fields/components for each tier |\n\n| Role-specific layouts | Optimized for each tier's workflow; reduces cognitive load | More maintenance overhead; changes must be replicated across layouts |\n\nRecommendation: use role-specific layouts when tiers have meaningfully different workflows (e.g., Tier 1 handles chat with simple scripts; Tier 3 handles escalations requiring technical detail). Use a universal layout when tiers share 80%+ of the same workflow."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s6_given_a_scenario_analyze_customer_requir_4", number: 4, question: "How Dynamic Forms and Dynamic Related Lists can be used in the Service Console to reduce page clutter and show context-relevant fields based on case record type or status", answer: "- Dynamic Forms: replace static page layouts with field sections that show/hide based on visibility rules. Example: show \"Root Cause\" and \"Engineering Notes\" fields only when Case Status = \"Escalated to Engineering.\"\n\n- Dynamic Related Lists: show/hide related lists based on record criteria. Example: show the \"Assets\" related list only for product-related case record types.\n\nBenefits: agents see only what is relevant to the current case state, reducing scroll and cognitive load. Requires Lightning pages (not available in Classic).\n\nDynamic Forms are configured in the Lightning App Builder per record type."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s6_given_a_scenario_analyze_customer_requir_5", number: 5, question: "When a client requires agents to handle multiple cases simultaneously without losing context, which Service Console features should be recommended and why", answer: "- Multi-tab subtabs: each open record gets its own subtab within the console; agents switch without losing state.\n\n- Split View: agents can monitor a list while actively working a case.\n\n- Utility Bar: persistent tools (macros, history, notes) are available regardless of which tab is active.\n\n- Work item preservation: if an agent navigates away, the Omni-Channel work item remains in their queue; they do not lose the assignment.\n\n- Screen Pop (CTI): when a call comes in, the matching case or contact opens automatically, reducing context-switching time."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s6_given_a_scenario_analyze_customer_requir_6", number: 6, question: "When a client needs agents to see a 360-degree customer view while working a case, how to configure Related Lists, activity timeline, and external data integration in the console", answer: "- Related Lists: add Contact, Account, open cases, purchase history, entitlements, and assets to the case page layout.\n\n- Activity Timeline: shows all emails, calls, tasks, and events related to the contact/account in chronological order.\n\n- External data: use Salesforce Connect (External Objects) to display real-time data from external ERP or order systems without migrating data into Salesforce.\n\n- Custom Lightning components: embed external system data in a panel within the console (e.g., an order lookup component that queries the ERP via Apex callout).\n\nThe goal is for the agent to never need to open a second system while working a case."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s6_given_a_scenario_analyze_customer_requir_7", number: 7, question: "How to configure case feed and feed filters to surface the most actionable information for agents handling technical support cases", answer: "Case Feed: the Chatter-based feed on a case record showing all activity (emails, comments, log calls, status changes) in a timeline.\n\nFeed Filters: allow agents to filter the feed by type (emails only, comments only, activities only). Configuration:\n\n- Enable Case Feed in Service Cloud Setup.\n\n- Add Feed Filters component to the page layout (in Classic) or use the Activity Timeline in Lightning.\n\n- Customize which feed actions are available (email, log a call, change status, internal note).\n\nBest practice: for technical support, ensure \"Internal Notes\" and \"Email\" are the primary feed actions, and that the feed is sorted newest-first so agents see the latest update immediately."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s6_given_a_scenario_analyze_customer_requir_8", number: 8, question: "How Quick Text, macros, and email templates differ in their use cases for improving agent productivity and response consistency", answer: "| Tool | Description | Best Use Case |\n\n|---|---|---|\n\n| Quick Text | Pre-written text snippets inserted anywhere (email, chat, notes) using a shortcut | Short, reusable phrases during live interactions (greetings, closings, common answers) |\n\n| Macros | Automated multi-step instructions (send email, update field, add comment) run with one click | Repetitive multi-action workflows in the console (e.g., \"Close case and send resolution email\") |\n\n| Email Templates | Pre-formatted email bodies with merge fields, stored for use in case emails | Structured email communications with consistent formatting and dynamic data |\n\nKey difference: Quick Text inserts text. Macros execute actions. Templates format emails. They can be used together (a macro can apply a template)."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s6_given_a_scenario_analyze_customer_requir_9", number: 9, question: "When agents are working in the field or on mobile devices, what Salesforce Mobile App capabilities and limitations must be considered for Service Cloud use cases", answer: "Capabilities:\n\n- View and update case records.\n\n- Log calls and add case comments.\n\n- Access Knowledge articles.\n\n- Receive push notifications for new case assignments.\n\n- Use offline access for records previously viewed (limited sync).\n\nLimitations:\n\n- Service Console multi-tab experience is NOT available on mobile (mobile uses standard record pages).\n\n- Macros are NOT supported in the mobile app.\n\n- Omni-Channel agent presence and routing are NOT fully supported on mobile.\n\n- CTI / softphone utility bar components do not function on mobile.\n\n- Complex page layouts may not render optimally.\n\nRecommendation: design a simplified mobile page layout for field agents. For full console functionality, agents need a desktop browser.")
                    ]
                )
            ]
        ),

        // Section 7: Given a scenario, analyze customer requirements to determine (18 topics)
        StudySection(
            id: "s7_given_a_scenario_analyze_customer_requir",
            title: "Given a scenario, analyze customer requirements to determine an appropriate solution design considering capabilities, limitations, and design trade-offs for the customer experience.",
            color: Color(red: 0.40, green: 0.60, blue: 0.30),
            lightColor: Color(red: 0.92, green: 0.96, blue: 0.90),
            icon: "leaf.fill",
            objectives: [
                StudyObjective(
                    id: "s7_given_a_scenario_analyze_customer_requir_obj",
                    title: "Given a scenario, analyze customer requirements to determine an appropriate solution design considering capabilities, limitations, and design trade-offs for the customer experience.",
                    topics: [
                        StudyTopic(id: "s7_given_a_scenario_analyze_customer_requir_1", number: 1, question: "How to configure email-to-case and web-to-case to ensure consistent customer experience across inbound contact channels", answer: "Email-to-Case:\n\n- Configure a dedicated support email address that forwards to Salesforce.\n\n- Use On-Demand Email-to-Case (recommended) to keep email processing within Salesforce servers.\n\n- Set up auto-response rules to acknowledge receipt immediately.\n\n- Configure assignment rules to route cases from email to the appropriate queue.\n\nWeb-to-Case:\n\n- Generate the HTML form in Setup and embed on the support website.\n\n- Map form fields to case fields (Subject, Description, Priority, Product).\n\n- Configure auto-response rules for immediate acknowledgment.\n\n- Limit field collection to what is necessary to avoid form abandonment.\n\nConsistency: both channels should produce cases with the same required fields populated, route to the same assignment rule logic, and trigger the same auto-response. Use a shared auto-response template."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s7_given_a_scenario_analyze_customer_requir_2", number: 2, question: "How to design a self-service portal using Experience Cloud that minimizes case creation volume while maintaining CSAT for customers who cannot resolve issues independently", answer: "Deflection-first design:"),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s7_given_a_scenario_analyze_customer_requir_3", number: 3, question: "**Prominent search bar**: search Knowledge articles before showing the case form.", answer: ""),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s7_given_a_scenario_analyze_customer_requir_4", number: 4, question: "**Article suggestions**: surface related articles as the customer types their issue.", answer: ""),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s7_given_a_scenario_analyze_customer_requir_5", number: 5, question: "**Agentforce bot**: intercept the customer before case form access; attempt to resolve via AI.", answer: ""),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s7_given_a_scenario_analyze_customer_requir_6", number: 6, question: "**Progressive case form**: if the customer still cannot resolve, offer the case form — but pre-populate fields where possible (logged-in user context).", answer: ""),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s7_given_a_scenario_analyze_customer_requir_7", number: 7, question: "**Community answers**: enable peer-to-peer Q&A so customers help each other.", answer: "CSAT maintenance: ensure that if self-service fails, escalation to a case is frictionless. Customers who hit a dead end and cannot create a case quickly will have very low CSAT."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s7_given_a_scenario_analyze_customer_requir_8", number: 8, question: "When a customer requires seamless escalation from a self-service chatbot to a live agent without repeating context, which Service Cloud features support this and what their limitations are", answer: "Features:\n\n- Agentforce AI Agent: when configured with human handoff, passes the full conversation transcript to the live agent in the Omni-Channel work item.\n\n- Messaging for In-App and Web (MIAW): conversation history is preserved in the messaging session when transferring to a live agent.\n\n- Einstein Bot (legacy): supports agent handoff with transcript transfer; requires Live Agent or MIAW.\n\nLimitations:\n\n- Context transfer is limited to the conversation transcript; the agent still must review it manually.\n\n- If the bot is on a different channel than the live agent queue (e.g., bot on web, agent on phone), context cannot be transferred automatically.\n\n- Authenticated vs. unauthenticated sessions affect how much pre-populated customer data is available to the receiving agent."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s7_given_a_scenario_analyze_customer_requir_9", number: 9, question: "What configuration is required to send automated case status update notifications to customers, and how to avoid over-notification fatigue", answer: "Configuration:\n\n- Email Alerts via Flow or Process Builder triggered on case Status change.\n\n- Notification templates: use email templates with merge fields (Case Number, Status, Next Steps).\n\n- Customer Portal notifications: Experience Cloud can show in-app notifications for case updates.\n\nAvoiding over-notification fatigue:\n\n- Only send notifications for meaningful status changes (e.g., \"In Progress\" → \"Waiting on Customer,\" \"Resolved\"). Do not notify on every internal status transition.\n\n- Consolidate: if multiple updates happen in quick succession, consider a short delay (batch window) before sending.\n\n- Allow customer preferences: let customers opt into/out of specific notification types.\n\n- Avoid notifying on auto-response acknowledgment AND status update within seconds of each other."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s7_given_a_scenario_analyze_customer_requir_10", number: 10, question: "How to design a post-case survey flow in Service Cloud to automatically collect CSAT scores after case closure", answer: "Design steps:"),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s7_given_a_scenario_analyze_customer_requir_11", number: 11, question: "Create a **Survey** in Salesforce Surveys with a CSAT question (1–5 scale) and optional open-text follow-up.", answer: ""),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s7_given_a_scenario_analyze_customer_requir_12", number: 12, question: "Build a **Record-Triggered Flow** on the Case object: trigger = Status changed to \"Closed.\"", answer: ""),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s7_given_a_scenario_analyze_customer_requir_13", number: 13, question: "Flow action: use \"Send Survey Invitation\" to send the survey via email to the case contact.", answer: ""),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s7_given_a_scenario_analyze_customer_requir_14", number: 14, question: "Set a **time delay** (e.g., 1 hour after closure) to avoid sending before the customer has seen the resolution.", answer: ""),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s7_given_a_scenario_analyze_customer_requir_15", number: 15, question: "Store survey responses linked to the Case record for reporting.", answer: ""),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s7_given_a_scenario_analyze_customer_requir_16", number: 16, question: "Build a **report** on Survey Response to calculate average CSAT score by agent, queue, or channel.", answer: ""),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s7_given_a_scenario_analyze_customer_requir_17", number: 17, question: "What configuration triggers the survey send in a post-case CSAT survey flow", answer: "The trigger is a Record-Triggered Flow on the Case object where the entry condition is:\n\n- Object: Case\n\n- Trigger: Record is Updated\n\n- Condition: Status equals \"Closed\" (AND Status was not previously \"Closed\" to prevent re-triggering on edits to already-closed cases)\n\nThe flow then uses a scheduled path (e.g., 1 hour after the trigger) to send the survey invitation, ensuring the customer has time to experience the resolution before receiving the survey."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s7_given_a_scenario_analyze_customer_requir_18", number: 18, question: "The design trade-offs between a fully branded Experience Cloud customer portal versus a third-party support website integrated via API for customer-facing support", answer: "| Approach | Pros | Cons |\n\n|---|---|---|\n\n| Experience Cloud portal | Native Salesforce integration; real-time case/Knowledge data; no integration overhead; lower long-term cost | Less design flexibility; Salesforce-hosted; branding limited to Experience Builder themes |\n\n| Third-party website + API | Full design freedom; existing web team can maintain it; can integrate other data sources | Integration complexity; API call limits; data sync latency; ongoing maintenance cost |\n\nRecommend Experience Cloud when: the company wants a fast, Salesforce-native self-service experience with minimal integration work.\n\nRecommend third-party when: the company has an existing web platform with significant investment and complex branding/UX requirements that Experience Builder cannot meet.")
                    ]
                )
            ]
        ),

        // Section 8: Given a set of customer data security and compliance require (6 topics)
        StudySection(
            id: "s8_given_a_set_of_customer_data_security_an",
            title: "Given a set of customer data security and compliance requirements, determine a successful design to secure the data.",
            color: Color(red: 0.50, green: 0.30, blue: 0.60),
            lightColor: Color(red: 0.94, green: 0.90, blue: 0.96),
            icon: "cpu.fill",
            objectives: [
                StudyObjective(
                    id: "s8_given_a_set_of_customer_data_security_an_obj",
                    title: "Given a set of customer data security and compliance requirements, determine a successful design to secure the data.",
                    topics: [
                        StudyTopic(id: "s8_given_a_set_of_customer_data_security_an_1", number: 1, question: "The difference between object-level, record-level, and field-level security in Salesforce", answer: "- Object-Level Security (OLS): controls whether a user can see the Case object at all (Read, Create, Edit, Delete, View All, Modify All). Set via Profiles and Permission Sets.\n\n- Record-Level Security: controls which specific Case records a user can access. Enforced by OWD, Role Hierarchy, Sharing Rules, and Manual Sharing.\n\n- Field-Level Security (FLS): controls whether a user can see or edit a specific field (e.g., SSN, Credit Card) on a record they already have access to. Set via Profiles and Permission Sets.\n\nThink of it as: OLS = can you enter the building? Record-level = which rooms can you enter? FLS = which drawers can you open?"),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s8_given_a_set_of_customer_data_security_an_2", number: 2, question: "When object-level, record-level, and field-level security must be applied together to meet a compliance requirement", answer: "Example: HIPAA compliance for a healthcare contact center.\n\n- OLS: only licensed clinical staff and support agents have Read access to the MedicalCase custom object.\n\n- Record-level: agents can only see cases assigned to their region (OWD = Private + role hierarchy sharing).\n\n- FLS: agents can see the case but cannot see the Diagnosis Code field; only clinical staff have FLS access to that field.\n\nAll three layers must be applied together because each layer addresses a different axis of access. Relying on only one layer leaves gaps."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s8_given_a_set_of_customer_data_security_an_3", number: 3, question: "How the Case OWD (Organization-Wide Default) setting interacts with role hierarchy, sharing rules, and queue membership to determine which agents can view and edit cases", answer: "OWD sets the baseline: if Case OWD = Private, users can only see cases they own by default.\n\nRole Hierarchy opens access upward: managers in a higher role than the case owner can see records owned by their subordinates.\n\nSharing Rules extend access horizontally: cases owned by users in Group A can be shared with users in Group B (e.g., cross-regional sharing), without changing OWD.\n\nQueue membership: members of a queue can see all cases assigned to that queue, regardless of OWD. Cases in a queue are owned by the queue, so queue members get access.\n\nThe layered model means OWD restricts, and each subsequent mechanism opens access selectively."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s8_given_a_set_of_customer_data_security_an_4", number: 4, question: "How to design record-level security for cases so that agents can only see cases assigned to their team, while managers can see all cases in their region", answer: "Design:\n\n- Set Case OWD = Private.\n\n- Create a Role Hierarchy with Region Manager above Team Lead above Agent.\n\n- Assign agents to their team lead's subordinate role; assign team leads to the region manager's subordinate role.\n\n- With O role hierarchy: agents see only their own cases; team leads see all cases owned by their agents; region managers see all cases in their region.\n\nNo sharing rules are needed for this basic structure. If agents need to collaborate across teams within a region, add sharing rules or use queues."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s8_given_a_set_of_customer_data_security_an_5", number: 5, question: "How to use sharing rules and manual sharing in Service Cloud to extend record visibility to cross-regional teams without opening the OWD", answer: "- Criteria-based sharing rules: share cases matching certain criteria (e.g., Case Region = \"West\") with a specific group or role. This does not change OWD — it only extends access for cases meeting the criteria.\n\n- Owner-based sharing rules: share all cases owned by users in Role A with users in Role B.\n\n- Manual Sharing: an agent or admin can manually share a specific case with another user or group on an ad-hoc basis. Uses the \"Sharing\" button on the record.\n\nBoth approaches extend visibility without opening OWD to Public Read or Public Read/Write, preserving the security model."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s8_given_a_set_of_customer_data_security_an_6", number: 6, question: "When field-level security must restrict agents from seeing customer PII on case records while still allowing access to case details, how to configure this in Salesforce", answer: "- Identify PII fields (e.g., SSN, Date of Birth, Credit Card Number).\n\n- For the agent Profile or Permission Set, set FLS on those fields to Hidden (no Read, no Edit access).\n\n- Agents will see the case record (OLS grants access) but PII fields will be blank and not visible.\n\n- For supervisor/compliance Permission Sets, grant Read (or Read/Edit) FLS on the same fields.\n\n- Use Salesforce Shield Platform Encryption to encrypt PII fields at rest for an additional security layer beyond FLS.\n\nFLS is applied at the profile/permission set level — it does not affect API access unless you also restrict API access separately.")
                    ]
                )
            ]
        ),

        // Section 9: Explain the Knowledge article lifecycle, Knowledge Centered  (33 topics)
        StudySection(
            id: "s9_explain_the_knowledge_article_lifecycle_",
            title: "Explain the Knowledge article lifecycle, Knowledge Centered Service (KCS), and Salesforce Knowledge Management configuration.",
            color: Color(red: 0.60, green: 0.50, blue: 0.20),
            lightColor: Color(red: 0.98, green: 0.96, blue: 0.88),
            icon: "lightbulb.fill",
            objectives: [
                StudyObjective(
                    id: "s9_explain_the_knowledge_article_lifecycle__obj",
                    title: "Explain the Knowledge article lifecycle, Knowledge Centered Service (KCS), and Salesforce Knowledge Management configuration.",
                    topics: [
                        StudyTopic(id: "s9_explain_the_knowledge_article_lifecycle__1", number: 1, question: "The stages of the Salesforce Knowledge article lifecycle from draft to published to archived", answer: ""),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s9_explain_the_knowledge_article_lifecycle__2", number: 2, question: "**Draft**: article is created and being authored. Not visible to agents or customers.", answer: ""),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s9_explain_the_knowledge_article_lifecycle__3", number: 3, question: "**In Review** (optional, via approval process): article submitted for editorial or compliance review.", answer: ""),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s9_explain_the_knowledge_article_lifecycle__4", number: 4, question: "**Published**: article is live and visible to permitted audiences (agents, customers, partners based on data category visibility).", answer: ""),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s9_explain_the_knowledge_article_lifecycle__5", number: 5, question: "**Archived**: article is removed from active search results but retained for historical reference. Linked cases are not affected; the link remains but the article is marked archived.", answer: "Articles can be versioned: editing a published article creates a new draft version while the current published version remains live until the new version is published."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s9_explain_the_knowledge_article_lifecycle__6", number: 6, question: "How Knowledge article versioning works in Salesforce", answer: "When you edit a published article, Salesforce creates a new draft version while keeping the currently published version live. Agents and customers continue to see the current version until the new version is published.\n\n- Each version has its own Version Number.\n\n- Publishing the new version archives the previous version automatically.\n\n- You can view version history on the article record.\n\n- Cases linked to an older version retain the link; the article detail page shows which version was attached.\n\nThis prevents disruption to agents working with current information while updates are being reviewed."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s9_explain_the_knowledge_article_lifecycle__7", number: 7, question: "How Knowledge Centered Service (KCS) methodology aligns with Salesforce Knowledge workflows", answer: "KCS is a methodology where knowledge is created and improved as a natural byproduct of resolving issues — not as a separate documentation project.\n\nAlignment with Salesforce Knowledge:\n\n- Capture: agents create draft articles from within the case (case deflection/knowledge capture components).\n\n- Structure: article record types enforce consistent format (symptoms, environment, resolution).\n\n- Reuse: agents search and attach existing articles to cases before creating new ones.\n\n- Improve: article ratings, feedback, and usage metrics trigger review workflows for stale content.\n\n- Retire: articles that are no longer relevant are archived.\n\nKCS emphasizes that every agent is a knowledge contributor, not just dedicated knowledge managers."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s9_explain_the_knowledge_article_lifecycle__8", number: 8, question: "What KCS principles should drive article creation governance", answer: "Core KCS principles:\n\n- Capture knowledge at the point of need: create or update articles during case resolution, not after.\n\n- Relevance over perfection: a good-enough article published quickly is more valuable than a perfect article published later.\n\n- Collective ownership: the whole team is responsible for knowledge quality; any agent can flag an article for improvement.\n\n- Demand-driven content: create articles for issues that recur; avoid creating speculative articles.\n\n- Review frequency tied to usage: high-traffic articles get reviewed more often; low-traffic articles are candidates for archiving.\n\nGovernance: use workflow/approval processes to enforce review cycles without creating bottlenecks."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s9_explain_the_knowledge_article_lifecycle__9", number: 9, question: "Which user roles can transition Knowledge articles between lifecycle states", answer: "Transitions depend on Knowledge permission sets and org configuration:\n\n| Permission | Can Do |\n\n|---|---|\n\n| Knowledge Author | Create and edit draft articles; submit for approval |\n\n| Knowledge Publisher | Publish, archive, and restore articles |\n\n| Knowledge Manager | Full control: create, edit, publish, archive, manage data categories and record types |\n\n| Knowledge Viewer | Read-only access to published articles |\n\nNote: \"Manage Salesforce Knowledge\" system permission is required for full admin control. Publication rights can also be granted via approval process final approval actions."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s9_explain_the_knowledge_article_lifecycle__10", number: 10, question: "The role of the Knowledge Manager, Author, Publisher, and Viewer permission sets in the article lifecycle and how they enforce governance", answer: "- Knowledge Manager: controls taxonomy (data categories, record types), monitors article quality metrics, manages approval processes. Serves as the governance owner.\n\n- Author: creates articles from case interactions; flags articles for review; cannot publish directly if an approval process is in place.\n\n- Publisher: reviews submitted articles for quality and accuracy; publishes or rejects drafts; ensures compliance before content goes live.\n\n- Viewer: agents or customers who can search and read published articles but cannot create or modify them.\n\nThis four-role model enforces a content governance workflow where creation and publication are separated, preventing low-quality articles from going live unchecked."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s9_explain_the_knowledge_article_lifecycle__11", number: 11, question: "How to configure multiple Knowledge article record types in Salesforce", answer: ""),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s9_explain_the_knowledge_article_lifecycle__12", number: 12, question: "Enable Salesforce Knowledge in Setup.", answer: ""),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s9_explain_the_knowledge_article_lifecycle__13", number: 13, question: "Navigate to Setup > Knowledge > Article Types (Classic) or Setup > Object Manager > Knowledge (Lightning) to create record types.", answer: ""),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s9_explain_the_knowledge_article_lifecycle__14", number: 14, question: "Create a record type for each article category (e.g., \"Internal Procedure,\" \"External FAQ,\" \"Product Release Note\").", answer: ""),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s9_explain_the_knowledge_article_lifecycle__15", number: 15, question: "Assign page layouts per record type to control which fields appear for each article type.", answer: ""),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s9_explain_the_knowledge_article_lifecycle__16", number: 16, question: "Assign record type access to profiles/permission sets as needed.", answer: "Each record type can have its own fields, page layout, and approval process, allowing different governance workflows per article category."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s9_explain_the_knowledge_article_lifecycle__17", number: 17, question: "When using separate record types for internal procedures versus external FAQs is preferable to a single article type", answer: "Use separate record types when:\n\n- Internal procedures have different fields than external FAQs (e.g., internal articles have \"Escalation Path\" field; external FAQs do not).\n\n- Different approval workflows apply (internal procedures approved by operations manager; external FAQs approved by legal/marketing).\n\n- Different audiences need different layouts (internal-facing vs. customer-facing content looks different).\n\n- You want to restrict data category visibility differently per article type.\n\nUse a single record type when: the content structure is identical and the only difference is audience visibility (controlled via data categories and channel visibility settings instead)."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s9_explain_the_knowledge_article_lifecycle__18", number: 18, question: "How to configure data categories in Salesforce Knowledge", answer: ""),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s9_explain_the_knowledge_article_lifecycle__19", number: 19, question: "Setup > Data Category Setup > Create a Category Group (e.g., \"Products,\" \"Regions\").", answer: ""),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s9_explain_the_knowledge_article_lifecycle__20", number: 20, question: "Add categories within each group (e.g., under \"Products\": \"Hardware,\" \"Software,\" \"Services\").", answer: ""),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s9_explain_the_knowledge_article_lifecycle__21", number: 21, question: "Assign the Category Group to Knowledge (and other objects like Cases, if needed).", answer: ""),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s9_explain_the_knowledge_article_lifecycle__22", number: 22, question: "On each Knowledge article, set the appropriate data category values.", answer: ""),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s9_explain_the_knowledge_article_lifecycle__23", number: 23, question: "Configure data category visibility on profiles/permission sets to control which users see which category of articles.", answer: "Data categories serve dual purpose: filtering/searching articles and controlling article visibility."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s9_explain_the_knowledge_article_lifecycle__24", number: 24, question: "Why category hierarchies affect both article visibility and search relevance in Salesforce Knowledge", answer: "Visibility: data category visibility settings cascade down the hierarchy. If a user has access to \"Software,\" they automatically see articles tagged with \"Software > CRM\" and \"Software > ERP\" (child categories). Restricting access to \"Software\" removes access to all child categories as well.\n\nSearch relevance: Salesforce Knowledge search uses data categories as a filter. Articles categorized under the user's accessible categories surface in search; articles outside their visibility do not appear, regardless of keyword match.\n\nImplication: designing a shallow category hierarchy makes articles easier to find; overly deep hierarchies fragment articles and reduce search relevance."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s9_explain_the_knowledge_article_lifecycle__25", number: 25, question: "How to configure article approval processes using Salesforce workflow or approval processes to ensure content quality before publishing", answer: ""),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s9_explain_the_knowledge_article_lifecycle__26", number: 26, question: "In Setup, go to **Approval Processes** for the Knowledge Article Version object.", answer: ""),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s9_explain_the_knowledge_article_lifecycle__27", number: 27, question: "Create an approval process with entry criteria (e.g., Status = \"In Review\" or a custom \"Ready for Review\" checkbox).", answer: ""),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s9_explain_the_knowledge_article_lifecycle__28", number: 28, question: "Define approver steps: single approver (Knowledge Publisher), or sequential/parallel approval with multiple reviewers.", answer: ""),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s9_explain_the_knowledge_article_lifecycle__29", number: 29, question: "Configure final approval action: set Status to \"Published\" (automatically publishes the article upon final approval).", answer: ""),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s9_explain_the_knowledge_article_lifecycle__30", number: 30, question: "Configure rejection action: return to draft with a rejection comment notification to the author.", answer: "This ensures no article goes live without human review, which is critical for compliance-heavy environments."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s9_explain_the_knowledge_article_lifecycle__31", number: 31, question: "How article feedback and ratings can be used to identify stale or low-quality knowledge articles and trigger review workflows", answer: "- Salesforce Knowledge supports thumbs up/down ratings and written feedback on articles.\n\n- Track aggregate ratings using Knowledge article reports (KnowledgeArticleViewStat, KnowledgeArticleVoteStat).\n\n- Set a threshold: articles with a vote ratio below (e.g., 60% positive) after a minimum vote count are flagged for review.\n\n- Use Record-Triggered Flows or scheduled reports to create a Task or chatter notification to the Knowledge Manager when an article falls below the quality threshold.\n\n- Article Feedback: agents can submit written feedback directly on an article, which the Knowledge Manager reviews."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s9_explain_the_knowledge_article_lifecycle__32", number: 32, question: "What happens to linked cases when a Knowledge article is archived or replaced by a new version", answer: "- Archived article: the article link on the case remains intact. The case record shows the article is attached, but the article is in \"Archived\" status. Agents can still navigate to the archived article and read it; it just no longer appears in active search results.\n\n- New version published: when a new version is published, the previous version is automatically archived. Case links that pointed to the old version now resolve to the archived version. They do not automatically update to the new version.\n\nTakeaway: archiving does not break case-article links. Links are preserved for historical accuracy and compliance purposes."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s9_explain_the_knowledge_article_lifecycle__33", number: 33, question: "What is required to import existing articles from a legacy knowledge base into Salesforce Knowledge, including the Knowledge Article CSV import process and its limitations", answer: "Requirements:\n\n- Articles must be formatted in CSV files with one file per article record type and separate CSV files for translations (if applicable).\n\n- One ZIP file containing the CSV and any associated file attachments.\n\n- Articles must map to existing Salesforce Knowledge record types and data categories.\n\nProcess: Setup > Knowledge > Article Import and Export > Import Articles.\n\nLimitations:\n\n- Maximum of 10,000 articles per import job.\n\n- File size limits per import ZIP.\n\n- Rich text formatting (HTML) can lose formatting during import.\n\n- Does not import article version history from the legacy system — only the current version is imported.\n\n- Data category values in the CSV must exactly match existing Salesforce data category names.")
                    ]
                )
            ]
        ),

        // Section 10: Given a set of requirements, determine how to configure Know (11 topics)
        StudySection(
            id: "s10_given_a_set_of_requirements_determine_ho",
            title: "Given a set of requirements, determine how to configure Knowledge to deliver service support and self-service processes.",
            color: Color(red: 0.35, green: 0.45, blue: 0.65),
            lightColor: Color(red: 0.90, green: 0.92, blue: 0.96),
            icon: "building.2.fill",
            objectives: [
                StudyObjective(
                    id: "s10_given_a_set_of_requirements_determine_ho_obj",
                    title: "Given a set of requirements, determine how to configure Knowledge to deliver service support and self-service processes.",
                    topics: [
                        StudyTopic(id: "s10_given_a_set_of_requirements_determine_ho_1", number: 1, question: "When agents need to search and attach Knowledge articles to cases from within the Service Console, what configuration is required and which components must be added to the page layout", answer: "Configuration:"),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s10_given_a_set_of_requirements_determine_ho_2", number: 2, question: "Enable Salesforce Knowledge in Setup.", answer: ""),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s10_given_a_set_of_requirements_determine_ho_3", number: 3, question: "Enable **Knowledge on Cases** (Setup > Knowledge Settings > Allow users to create and edit articles from the Case Detail page).", answer: ""),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s10_given_a_set_of_requirements_determine_ho_4", number: 4, question: "Add the **Knowledge component** (Lightning component) to the Case Lightning record page in the App Builder.", answer: ""),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s10_given_a_set_of_requirements_determine_ho_5", number: 5, question: "Ensure agents have at least the Knowledge Viewer permission set.", answer: "Components:\n\n- Knowledge Component: the main search and attach widget on the case page. Agents type keywords and see suggested articles.\n\n- Suggested Articles: auto-surfaces articles based on case fields (Subject, Description) as soon as the case is saved.\n\nWithout the Knowledge component on the page layout, agents must navigate away from the case to find articles."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s10_given_a_set_of_requirements_determine_ho_6", number: 6, question: "How to use the Knowledge Lightning component versus the Knowledge sidebar to surface relevant articles based on case fields and search terms", answer: "Knowledge Lightning Component (modern, Lightning Experience):\n\n- Embedded on the case record page via App Builder.\n\n- Surfaces suggested articles based on case Subject and Description using search indexing.\n\n- Agents can search, preview, and attach articles without leaving the case.\n\n- Configurable to show specific article types.\n\nKnowledge Sidebar (legacy, Salesforce Classic):\n\n- A sidebar panel in the Classic console that auto-searches based on case Subject.\n\n- Less configurable than the Lightning component.\n\nFor Lightning-based implementations, use the Knowledge Lightning Component. The sidebar is a Classic-era feature and should not be the primary recommendation for modern implementations."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s10_given_a_set_of_requirements_determine_ho_7", number: 7, question: "How data category visibility and role hierarchy interact to determine which users see which published Knowledge articles in an org with multiple customer segments", answer: "- Data category visibility is set per profile or permission set — not per role.\n\n- If a user's profile grants visibility to the \"Enterprise\" data category, they see all articles tagged \"Enterprise\" regardless of their position in the role hierarchy.\n\n- Role hierarchy does NOT cascade data category visibility (unlike record sharing). A manager does not automatically see categories their subordinates can see unless the manager's profile/permission set explicitly grants that visibility.\n\n- In an org with multiple segments (e.g., SMB, Enterprise, Partner), create separate data categories and assign visibility per profile/permission set to match each agent group's customer segment."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s10_given_a_set_of_requirements_determine_ho_8", number: 8, question: "How to expose Knowledge articles to external customers through an Experience Cloud portal while restricting internal-only articles from being visible", answer: "- Use Channel visibility settings on each article: mark internal articles as visible to \"Internal App\" only; mark external FAQs as visible to \"Public\" or \"Customer\" channels.\n\n- In the Experience Cloud site, the Knowledge component surfaces only articles marked for the \"Customer\" or \"Public\" channel.\n\n- Additionally, use data categories: assign internal articles to a data category that has no visibility grant for the Experience Cloud guest user or authenticated customer profile.\n\nBoth channel visibility and data category visibility should be used together for defense-in-depth control over article exposure."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s10_given_a_set_of_requirements_determine_ho_9", number: 9, question: "The design trade-offs between a single article type versus multiple article types in a Knowledge implementation for a company with both internal procedures and external FAQs", answer: "See also: Section on record types. The key trade-offs:\n\n| | Single Article Type | Multiple Article Types |\n\n|---|---|---|\n\n| Maintenance | Simpler; one page layout, one approval process | More complex; separate layouts and processes per type |\n\n| Governance | Harder to enforce different standards per audience | Easier to apply type-specific approval workflows |\n\n| Agent UX | Agents may see irrelevant fields | Fields are tailored to content purpose |\n\n| Reporting | Harder to segment article performance by type | Easy to filter reports by record type |\n\nRecommendation: use multiple record types when content structure, governance, or audience differs meaningfully. Use a single type only for simple, homogeneous knowledge bases."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s10_given_a_set_of_requirements_determine_ho_10", number: 10, question: "When a company wants to use AI-suggested Knowledge articles to assist agents in real time during a chat interaction, what must be configured in Service Cloud", answer: "- Enable Einstein for Service (requires appropriate Service Cloud Einstein license).\n\n- Enable Einstein Article Recommendations in Setup > Einstein > Article Recommendations.\n\n- Train the model: requires a history of cases where articles were attached (minimum data volume required — typically 1,000+ resolved cases with article attachments).\n\n- Add the Einstein Recommendations component to the messaging/chat console page layout.\n\n- During a live chat, Einstein analyzes the conversation and surfaces recommended articles in real time, which agents can share with the customer with one click.\n\nWithout sufficient case+article attachment history, the AI model cannot make quality recommendations."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s10_given_a_set_of_requirements_determine_ho_11", number: 11, question: "How to track Knowledge article usage metrics — views, attachments to cases, and thumbs up/down ratings — and use them to demonstrate ROI of the Knowledge investment", answer: "Metrics to track:\n\n- Views: reported via KnowledgeArticleViewStat (report type: Knowledge Articles).\n\n- Case attachments: cases with a related Knowledge article (use Articles & Cases report type or custom report).\n\n- Thumbs up/down: KnowledgeArticleVoteStat object.\n\n- Deflection: cases NOT created after a knowledge article view in the Experience Cloud portal (measured via Analytics or custom tracking).\n\nROI calculation:\n\n- AHT reduction: before vs. after knowledge deployment.\n\n- Deflection count x average cost-per-contact = cost savings.\n\n- FCR improvement x volume = repeat contact cost avoidance.\n\nBuild a Salesforce Dashboard with components for each metric and present to leadership quarterly.")
                    ]
                )
            ]
        ),

        // Section 11: Given business process requirements, recommend the appropria (12 topics)
        StudySection(
            id: "s11_given_business_process_requirements_reco",
            title: "Given business process requirements, recommend the appropriate approach to intake channels and their design.",
            color: Color(red: 0.30, green: 0.50, blue: 0.80),
            lightColor: Color(red: 0.90, green: 0.94, blue: 1.00),
            icon: "doc.text.fill",
            objectives: [
                StudyObjective(
                    id: "s11_given_business_process_requirements_reco_obj",
                    title: "Given business process requirements, recommend the appropriate approach to intake channels and their design.",
                    topics: [
                        StudyTopic(id: "s11_given_business_process_requirements_reco_1", number: 1, question: "How to choose between Email-to-Case, Web-to-Case, and the Salesforce-native case submission form based on volume, routing complexity, and automation requirements", answer: "| Channel | Best When |\n\n|---|---|\n\n| Email-to-Case | Customers primarily use email; high volume of inbound email; need email threading (replies append to existing case) |\n\n| Web-to-Case | Customers submit issues via a web form; need structured data capture (dropdowns, required fields); max 5,000 cases/day |\n\n| Native Case Submission (Experience Cloud) | Authenticated customer portal users; need real-time data (existing cases, entitlements visible); richer form logic via Flows |\n\nRouting complexity: all three support assignment rules. Automation: all three support auto-response rules. The key differentiator is channel (email vs. web) and whether customers are authenticated."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s11_given_business_process_requirements_reco_2", number: 2, question: "The difference between On-Demand Email-to-Case and standard Email-to-Case, and when each should be used based on email volume and firewall constraints", answer: "Standard Email-to-Case: requires opening an inbound port on the company's email server to allow Salesforce to receive emails directly. Requires firewall configuration. Appropriate when the company controls email infrastructure and can open ports.\n\nOn-Demand Email-to-Case: emails are forwarded to a Salesforce-hosted email service (no firewall port changes required). Salesforce processes the email and creates the case. Preferred approach for most implementations because it does not require firewall changes and is more secure.\n\nKey limit: On-Demand Email-to-Case has a daily email processing limit (standard is 2,500/day; can be increased). Standard Email-to-Case does not have this limit but requires infrastructure access."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s11_given_business_process_requirements_reco_3", number: 3, question: "The design considerations when building a Web-to-Case form, including field mapping, auto-response rules, and case assignment rules", answer: "- Field mapping: only capture fields that are necessary for routing and resolution. Every additional field increases form abandonment.\n\n- Required fields: Subject and Description are essential. Priority, Product, and Case Type improve routing accuracy.\n\n- Auto-response rules: configure a rule that sends an email acknowledgment to the customer immediately upon case creation. Include case number and expected response time in the email.\n\n- Assignment rules: create criteria-based rules that route the case to the correct queue (e.g., Product = \"Hardware\" → Hardware Support Queue).\n\n- Hidden fields: pass additional metadata (e.g., page URL the customer submitted from) as hidden fields to aid routing without cluttering the form.\n\n- CAPTCHA / spam prevention: add reCAPTCHA to prevent automated spam submissions."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s11_given_business_process_requirements_reco_4", number: 4, question: "How to prevent spam when configuring a Web-to-Case form", answer: "- Add reCAPTCHA: Salesforce Web-to-Case supports Google reCAPTCHA integration. Humans pass; bots fail.\n\n- Required field validation: make Subject and Description required, with minimum character counts, to deter automated submissions.\n\n- Assignment rule filtering: create an assignment rule that routes cases with known spam patterns (certain keywords, invalid email formats) to a Spam queue for human review.\n\n- Flow-based validation: use a Screen Flow for case submission instead of the raw Web-to-Case form; Flows support complex validation logic.\n\n- IP rate limiting: implement at the web layer (not Salesforce) to limit submissions from a single IP."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s11_given_business_process_requirements_reco_5", number: 5, question: "When a company receives support requests through multiple channels (email, phone, chat, social), how to design an Omni-Channel routing strategy that routes to the right queue with the right priority", answer: "Design steps:"),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s11_given_business_process_requirements_reco_6", number: 6, question: "**Define Service Channels**: create one Service Channel per work item type (Case, Chat, Messaging Session, Social Post).", answer: ""),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s11_given_business_process_requirements_reco_7", number: 7, question: "**Create Queues**: one queue per team/skill group (Tier 1 Technical, Billing, Enterprise Support, etc.).", answer: ""),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s11_given_business_process_requirements_reco_8", number: 8, question: "**Configure Routing Configurations**: set capacity and priority per channel per queue. Example: Chat has priority 10 (highest) because it is synchronous; Email has priority 1.", answer: ""),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s11_given_business_process_requirements_reco_9", number: 9, question: "**Assign agents to queues**: agents in a queue receive work from that queue when available.", answer: ""),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s11_given_business_process_requirements_reco_10", number: 10, question: "**Set Presence Configurations**: define which channels an agent can handle and their capacity (e.g., 1 chat + 3 email simultaneously).", answer: ""),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s11_given_business_process_requirements_reco_11", number: 11, question: "**Peak volume**: use queue overflow rules or chatbots to handle volume when agent capacity is exceeded.", answer: ""),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s11_given_business_process_requirements_reco_12", number: 12, question: "How to configure routing rules in Omni-Channel to handle peak volume spikes without overwhelming agents in a specific queue", answer: "- Capacity limits: set agent capacity per channel to prevent overload (e.g., max 2 concurrent chats per agent).\n\n- Queue-based overflow: if a queue's wait time exceeds a threshold, route new items to a secondary queue or trigger a bot interaction.\n\n- Skill-based overflow: expand the eligible agent pool during peak hours by temporarily including agents with adjacent skills.\n\n- Chatbot escalation buffer: deploy a bot to handle tier-1 interactions during peak volume; only escalate to agents when the bot cannot resolve.\n\n- Workforce management: schedule additional agents during predictable peak hours rather than relying solely on routing configuration to manage load.")
                    ]
                )
            ]
        ),

        // Section 12: Given a set of business goals, discuss the use cases and fun (19 topics)
        StudySection(
            id: "s12_given_a_set_of_business_goals_discuss_th",
            title: "Given a set of business goals, discuss the use cases and functionality for proposed interaction channels.",
            color: Color(red: 0.20, green: 0.60, blue: 0.50),
            lightColor: Color(red: 0.88, green: 0.96, blue: 0.94),
            icon: "checkmark.shield.fill",
            objectives: [
                StudyObjective(
                    id: "s12_given_a_set_of_business_goals_discuss_th_obj",
                    title: "Given a set of business goals, discuss the use cases and functionality for proposed interaction channels.",
                    topics: [
                        StudyTopic(id: "s12_given_a_set_of_business_goals_discuss_th_1", number: 1, question: "How voice integration via Service Cloud Voice works and what telephony options are available (Amazon Connect, partner telephony, Bring Your Own Telephony)", answer: "Service Cloud Voice (SCV) embeds telephony directly into the Service Console so agents handle calls within Salesforce without switching to a separate phone application. It provides real-time call controls, transcription, and screen pop.\n\nTelephony options:\n\n- Amazon Connect (Salesforce-provided): fully managed telephony through Salesforce. No separate telephony contract; Salesforce provisions and manages the Amazon Connect instance. Easiest to set up.\n\n- Partner Telephony: a Salesforce-certified telephony partner's cloud platform is connected to SCV. More flexibility in provider choice; requires partner setup.\n\n- Bring Your Own Telephony (BYOT): the company's existing telephony system is connected via API/CTI. Maximum flexibility; requires the most integration work."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s12_given_a_set_of_business_goals_discuss_th_2", number: 2, question: "When Amazon Connect, partner telephony, or Bring Your Own Telephony is the appropriate choice for Service Cloud Voice", answer: "| Choice | Best When |\n\n|---|---|\n\n| Amazon Connect | Starting fresh; no existing telephony investment; want Salesforce to manage the stack; fastest time to value |\n\n| Partner Telephony | Prefer a specific certified partner (e.g., Genesys, Avaya via SCV); need advanced features the partner provides |\n\n| BYOT | Significant existing telephony investment (on-premise PBX or cloud telephony); cannot or will not replace current phone system |\n\nCost and complexity: Amazon Connect < Partner Telephony < BYOT (in terms of integration effort)."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s12_given_a_set_of_business_goals_discuss_th_3", number: 3, question: "When a company wants to offer both synchronous (chat) and asynchronous (messaging) support, what Service Cloud channels support each and what distinguishes them operationally", answer: "Synchronous channels in Service Cloud:\n\n- Live Agent / Embedded Chat: real-time chat; session ends when either party disconnects.\n\n- Service Cloud Voice: real-time phone calls.\n\nAsynchronous channels:\n\n- Messaging for In-App and Web (MIAW): persistent messaging; customer can leave and return; conversation history preserved.\n\n- SMS/WhatsApp (via Messaging channels): mobile-first async messaging.\n\nOperational distinction: synchronous requires dedicated agent availability; agents handle one interaction at a time (or a small number concurrently). Asynchronous allows agents to manage more conversations because responses are not expected immediately."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s12_given_a_set_of_business_goals_discuss_th_4", number: 4, question: "The use cases for Salesforce Messaging for In-App and Web (MIAW) versus classic Live Agent chat, including handoff to agent and session persistence", answer: "| | MIAW | Classic Live Agent |\n\n|---|---|---|\n\n| Session persistence | Yes — conversation continues even if the customer closes the window | No — session ends when either party disconnects |\n\n| Channel support | In-app, web, SMS, WhatsApp, Facebook Messenger | Web only |\n\n| Async support | Yes | No (synchronous only) |\n\n| Agent handoff | Full transcript passed to agent | Transcript passed, but session must be active |\n\n| Bot integration | Agentforce / Einstein Bots integrate natively | Einstein Bots supported but older architecture |\n\n| Salesforce direction | Current investment; preferred | Legacy; being phased toward MIAW |\n\nRecommendation: for new implementations, use MIAW. Classic Live Agent is maintained but Salesforce's strategic direction is MIAW."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s12_given_a_set_of_business_goals_discuss_th_5", number: 5, question: "How Messaging channels (SMS/text, WhatsApp) are enabled in Service Cloud and what configuration steps differentiate messaging setup from chat channel setup", answer: "SMS/WhatsApp setup:"),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s12_given_a_set_of_business_goals_discuss_th_6", number: 6, question: "In Setup, go to **Messaging** (under Messaging Settings).", answer: ""),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s12_given_a_set_of_business_goals_discuss_th_7", number: 7, question: "Create a Messaging Channel (select SMS or WhatsApp).", answer: ""),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s12_given_a_set_of_business_goals_discuss_th_8", number: 8, question: "For WhatsApp: connect a Facebook Business Manager account and a WhatsApp Business Account.", answer: ""),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s12_given_a_set_of_business_goals_discuss_th_9", number: 9, question: "For SMS: provision a phone number via a supported provider (Salesforce-provided or Twilio).", answer: ""),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s12_given_a_set_of_business_goals_discuss_th_10", number: 10, question: "Create a **Service Channel** for Messaging in Omni-Channel.", answer: ""),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s12_given_a_set_of_business_goals_discuss_th_11", number: 11, question: "Create a **Routing Configuration** and assign to a Queue.", answer: ""),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s12_given_a_set_of_business_goals_discuss_th_12", number: 12, question: "Enable agents to handle messaging work items via **Presence Configuration**.", answer: "Key difference from chat: chat uses an Embedded Service Deployment (a JavaScript snippet on a website). Messaging uses a phone number or social account as the entry point, not a website embed."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s12_given_a_set_of_business_goals_discuss_th_13", number: 13, question: "The use cases for social media as a service channel, including how Social Customer Service connects social mentions to case creation and agent workflows", answer: "Use cases:\n\n- Monitor Twitter/X, Facebook for brand mentions and customer complaints.\n\n- Create cases automatically from social posts that match keywords (e.g., your brand name + negative sentiment).\n\n- Allow agents to respond to social posts from within the Service Console.\n\nHow Social Customer Service works:"),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s12_given_a_set_of_business_goals_discuss_th_14", number: 14, question: "Connect social accounts (Facebook Page, Twitter/X account) in Setup > Social Customer Service.", answer: ""),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s12_given_a_set_of_business_goals_discuss_th_15", number: 15, question: "Configure keyword matching rules to determine which posts create cases.", answer: ""),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s12_given_a_set_of_business_goals_discuss_th_16", number: 16, question: "Cases are created with the social post content in the Description; the customer's social profile is linked as a Contact.", answer: ""),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s12_given_a_set_of_business_goals_discuss_th_17", number: 17, question: "Agents respond to the case using the Feed Action, which posts the reply back to the original social thread.", answer: "Limitation: social channel volume can be high; filtering and keyword rules are critical to avoid creating noise cases."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s12_given_a_set_of_business_goals_discuss_th_18", number: 18, question: "When a company wants to reduce inbound case volume by enabling community-based peer support, how Experience Cloud communities can serve as a deflection channel", answer: "- Deploy an Experience Cloud community with a Q&A or discussion forum component (Chatter Questions).\n\n- Customers post questions; other community members (including expert customers and employees) answer.\n\n- Answered questions become searchable content that deflects future contacts.\n\n- Questions that community members cannot answer are escalated to a case with the conversation history intact.\n\n- Salesforce Knowledge articles can be surfaced alongside community answers to amplify deflection.\n\nMeasurement: track the ratio of questions answered by the community vs. escalated to cases to calculate community deflection rate."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s12_given_a_set_of_business_goals_discuss_th_19", number: 19, question: "The difference between an Einstein Bot / Flow Bot and an Agentforce AI Agent in terms of configuration approach, conversation model, and suitable use cases", answer: "| | Einstein Bot / Flow Bot | Agentforce AI Agent |\n\n|---|---|---|\n\n| Configuration | Dialog-tree or Flow-based; explicitly authored conversation paths | Topic + action-based; AI reasons over topics and decides which action to take |\n\n| Conversation model | Scripted; follows defined paths; limited by authored dialogs | Generative; understands natural language; can handle variations without explicit scripting |\n\n| Flexibility | Low — every variation must be scripted | High — handles novel phrasings within topic scope |\n\n| Suitable use cases | Structured, predictable interactions (hours inquiry, reset password via exact steps) | Complex, variable interactions (troubleshooting, multi-step processes, personalized responses) |\n\n| Guardrails | Defined by dialog design | Defined by topic scope, action permissions, and grounding configuration |\n\nAgentforce is Salesforce's strategic direction. Einstein Bots remain valid for simple, structured use cases.")
                    ]
                )
            ]
        ),

        // Section 13: Assess the design considerations and best practices when con (23 topics)
        StudySection(
            id: "s13_assess_the_design_considerations_and_bes",
            title: "Assess the design considerations and best practices when configuring an interaction channel solution.",
            color: Color(red: 0.55, green: 0.35, blue: 0.75),
            lightColor: Color(red: 0.94, green: 0.90, blue: 0.98),
            icon: "gearshape.2.fill",
            objectives: [
                StudyObjective(
                    id: "s13_assess_the_design_considerations_and_bes_obj",
                    title: "Assess the design considerations and best practices when configuring an interaction channel solution.",
                    topics: [
                        StudyTopic(id: "s13_assess_the_design_considerations_and_bes_1", number: 1, question: "How Service Channels and Routing Configurations must be set up for each work type (case, chat, messaging) before Omni-Channel can route that work item type", answer: "Required setup per work type:"),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s13_assess_the_design_considerations_and_bes_2", number: 2, question: "**Service Channel**: defines the Salesforce object that generates work items (e.g., Case, ChatTranscript, MessagingSession). One Service Channel per work type.", answer: ""),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s13_assess_the_design_considerations_and_bes_3", number: 3, question: "**Routing Configuration**: defines the priority, capacity, and routing model (Queue-Based or Skills-Based) for that channel. Each queue is assigned a Routing Configuration.", answer: ""),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s13_assess_the_design_considerations_and_bes_4", number: 4, question: "**Queue**: agents are members of queues. The queue is linked to the Routing Configuration.", answer: ""),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s13_assess_the_design_considerations_and_bes_5", number: 5, question: "**Presence Configuration**: defines which Service Channels an agent can be present on simultaneously and their capacity limits.", answer: ""),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s13_assess_the_design_considerations_and_bes_6", number: 6, question: "**Agent Presence**: agents must set themselves as \"Available\" for the appropriate channel in the Omni-Channel widget.", answer: "If any step is missing (e.g., no Service Channel for MessagingSession), Omni-Channel cannot route messaging work items."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s13_assess_the_design_considerations_and_bes_7", number: 7, question: "The configuration implications of enabling Enhanced Omni-Channel versus Classic Omni-Channel, and what migration considerations apply", answer: "Classic Omni-Channel: original routing engine; supports basic queue-based routing with capacity models.\n\nEnhanced Omni-Channel: next-generation routing engine with:\n\n- More granular capacity management.\n\n- Support for secondary routing criteria.\n\n- Better Skills-Based Routing integration.\n\n- Required for some newer features (e.g., Unified Routing with Agentforce).\n\nMigration considerations:\n\n- Enabling Enhanced Omni-Channel is a one-way change in some orgs — cannot easily revert.\n\n- Existing routing configurations and queues must be validated post-migration.\n\n- Test all routing scenarios in a sandbox before enabling in production.\n\n- Enhanced routing uses a different capacity model; agent capacity limits may need reconfiguration."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s13_assess_the_design_considerations_and_bes_8", number: 8, question: "What best practices should be followed when configuring Omni-Channel capacity models to balance agent workload and customer wait times", answer: "- Set realistic capacity per channel: do not assign more concurrent work items than an agent can handle with quality (e.g., max 2 concurrent chats for most agents).\n\n- Weight channels differently: synchronous work (chat) counts more toward capacity than async (email). Use relative weights.\n\n- Monitor utilization: use Omni Supervisor to watch real-time agent utilization; adjust capacity if agents are consistently over or under-loaded.\n\n- Test with realistic volume: load test routing in a sandbox before go-live to validate capacity settings at scale.\n\n- Include transfer/wrap-up time: account for after-chat work time in capacity models so agents are not immediately routed new items while completing wrap-up."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s13_assess_the_design_considerations_and_bes_9", number: 9, question: "The design considerations for routing work items using Omni-Channel Skills-Based Routing versus Queue-Based Routing and when skills-based is preferred", answer: "| | Queue-Based Routing | Skills-Based Routing |\n\n|---|---|---|\n\n| How it works | Routes work items to a queue; any available queue member can receive the item | Routes to an agent whose profile matches the required skills |\n\n| Configuration | Simpler; Queues + Routing Config | More complex; define Skills, assign agents skills with proficiency levels |\n\n| Best for | Generalist agent teams; simple routing by product or region | Specialized agents; multi-language support; technical certifications |\n\n| When skills-based is preferred | When matching the customer's language, issue type, or product expertise to an agent is more important than simply distributing load |\n\nSkills-based adds setup complexity but significantly improves FCR and CSAT when agent specialization matters."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s13_assess_the_design_considerations_and_bes_10", number: 10, question: "What the Omni-Channel Supervisor panel provides and how supervisors use it to monitor real-time agent status, queue backlog, and work item wait times", answer: "The Omni-Channel Supervisor (accessible via the Service Console utility bar or as a standalone app) provides:\n\n- Agent Status: real-time view of each agent's presence status (Available, Busy, Offline, custom statuses).\n\n- Agent Work Items: what each agent is currently handling.\n\n- Queue Backlog: how many work items are waiting in each queue.\n\n- Work Item Wait Times: how long each queued item has been waiting.\n\n- Channel breakdown: view metrics by channel (cases vs. chat vs. messaging).\n\nSupervisors use it to: reassign items from overloaded queues, flag agents who have been idle too long, and adjust staffing in real time during peak volume events."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s13_assess_the_design_considerations_and_bes_11", number: 11, question: "How to design auto-response rules for Email-to-Case to acknowledge receipt without creating agent confusion from customer replies to auto-responses", answer: "- Use a no-reply email address as the sender of auto-response emails (e.g., noreply@support.company.com). This prevents customer replies from creating additional emails on the case.\n\n- Do not use the agent's email or the support queue email as the From address of the auto-response.\n\n- Include the case number and a link to the portal in the auto-response so customers can check status without replying.\n\n- Set a clear message: \"Thank you for contacting us. Your case #XXXXX has been created. An agent will respond within [timeframe]. Please do not reply to this email.\"\n\n- Test threading: confirm that auto-responses are not creating new cases due to email threading issues (the In-Reply-To header must match for threading to work)."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s13_assess_the_design_considerations_and_bes_12", number: 12, question: "How to configure Embedded Service Deployments for chat, including pre-chat forms, offline support, and branding, and how this differs from a Chat Button configuration", answer: "Embedded Service Deployment (modern):"),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s13_assess_the_design_considerations_and_bes_13", number: 13, question: "Setup > Embedded Service Deployments > New Deployment (Chat type).", answer: ""),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s13_assess_the_design_considerations_and_bes_14", number: 14, question: "Configure: branding (colors, logo, name), availability (online/offline hours).", answer: ""),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s13_assess_the_design_considerations_and_bes_15", number: 15, question: "Add **Pre-Chat form**: fields the customer fills before connecting to an agent (Name, Email, Issue Type). These map to case fields and aid routing.", answer: ""),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s13_assess_the_design_considerations_and_bes_16", number: 16, question: "Set **Offline support**: when no agents are available, redirect to case form or display a message.", answer: ""),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s13_assess_the_design_considerations_and_bes_17", number: 17, question: "Generate a JavaScript snippet and embed on the company's website.", answer: "Chat Button (Classic): a simpler configuration that creates a button on a Visualforce page or website; less feature-rich; does not support pre-chat forms or branding as easily.\n\nFor new implementations, use Embedded Service Deployments. Chat Buttons are the legacy approach."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s13_assess_the_design_considerations_and_bes_18", number: 18, question: "How pre-chat data can be used to pre-populate case fields and speed routing in Messaging for In-App and Web", answer: "- During the pre-chat phase of a MIAW interaction, customers submit a form (e.g., Name, Email, Issue Type).\n\n- In Setup > Messaging Channels > Pre-Chat configuration, map pre-chat form fields to standard case fields (Subject, Case Type, Priority).\n\n- When the messaging session is created, these fields are auto-populated on the case, saving agent time.\n\n- Routing use: map Issue Type to a case field, then create an Omni-Channel routing rule based on that field value to route the session to the appropriate queue automatically.\n\n- For authenticated customers, pre-populate pre-chat fields using their profile data so they do not need to re-enter information they have already provided."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s13_assess_the_design_considerations_and_bes_19", number: 19, question: "How pre-chat and post-chat survey configuration works in Messaging for In-App and Web", answer: "Pre-chat: configured as a form within the MIAW channel settings. Fields are presented to the customer before the agent connection is made. Data captured maps to case/messaging session fields.\n\nPost-chat (post-conversation) survey:"),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s13_assess_the_design_considerations_and_bes_20", number: 20, question: "Create a **Salesforce Survey** with CSAT or NPS questions.", answer: ""),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s13_assess_the_design_considerations_and_bes_21", number: 21, question: "In the MIAW channel configuration, enable the post-conversation survey and link the Survey.", answer: ""),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s13_assess_the_design_considerations_and_bes_22", number: 22, question: "After the messaging session ends (agent marks it complete), the survey is automatically sent to the customer.", answer: ""),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s13_assess_the_design_considerations_and_bes_23", number: 23, question: "Responses are stored in the Survey Response object, linked to the messaging session and contact.", answer: "The key configuration point: the survey is triggered by session closure, not by a separate flow, when configured natively within the MIAW channel settings.")
                    ]
                )
            ]
        ),

        // Section 14: Given a set of business requirements, recommend an appropria (3 topics)
        StudySection(
            id: "s14_given_a_set_of_business_requirements_rec",
            title: "Given a set of business requirements, recommend an appropriate use for AI agents in the customer service process.",
            color: Color(red: 0.80, green: 0.40, blue: 0.20),
            lightColor: Color(red: 1.00, green: 0.94, blue: 0.88),
            icon: "chart.bar.xaxis.ascending",
            objectives: [
                StudyObjective(
                    id: "s14_given_a_set_of_business_requirements_rec_obj",
                    title: "Given a set of business requirements, recommend an appropriate use for AI agents in the customer service process.",
                    topics: [
                        StudyTopic(id: "s14_given_a_set_of_business_requirements_rec_1", number: 1, question: "What requirements must be met before AI agents can reliably resolve a class of customer service requests, including data quality, knowledge coverage, and action permissions", answer: "Prerequisites:\n\n- Data quality: customer and account data must be accurate and complete so the AI agent can identify the customer and retrieve correct information.\n\n- Knowledge coverage: the Knowledge base must cover the issue types the AI will handle. Gaps in articles lead to incorrect or hallucinated responses.\n\n- Action permissions: the AI agent must have permission to execute the actions required (e.g., look up order status via API, create cases, update records). Permissions must be scoped appropriately.\n\n- Well-defined scope: the AI agent should handle a specific, well-bounded set of request types. Overly broad scope increases the risk of errors.\n\n- Escalation path: a clear, tested handoff to a human agent must exist for cases the AI cannot resolve.\n\n- Sufficient volume: there must be enough volume for the issue type to justify AI automation; otherwise, cost savings do not materialize."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s14_given_a_set_of_business_requirements_rec_2", number: 2, question: "When a company wants to automate tier-1 case resolution for password resets and order status inquiries, when is an Agentforce AI agent the right recommendation versus a traditional bot", answer: "Agentforce AI agent is right when:\n\n- The request type has natural language variation (customers phrase the same request many different ways).\n\n- Resolution requires reasoning across multiple steps or data sources (e.g., look up order, check status, determine eligibility for resolution action).\n\n- The company wants the AI to improve over time with usage.\n\nTraditional bot is right when:\n\n- The interaction is highly structured and predictable (exact step-by-step process with no variation).\n\n- A decision tree accurately represents all possible paths.\n\n- The company lacks the Knowledge base or data quality required for generative AI grounding.\n\nFor password resets (simple, predictable) a Flow Bot may be sufficient. For order status (variable customer queries, multiple resolution paths) Agentforce AI is the better fit."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s14_given_a_set_of_business_requirements_rec_3", number: 3, question: "How to determine the appropriate handoff point from an AI agent to a human agent, and what data should be passed at escalation to preserve customer context", answer: "Handoff triggers:\n\n- AI agent cannot match the customer's request to a known topic after a defined number of attempts.\n\n- Customer explicitly requests a human agent.\n\n- The issue type is flagged as requiring human judgment (e.g., complaints, legal matters, complex billing disputes).\n\n- Sentiment detection identifies an angry or distressed customer.\n\n- The AI agent reaches a configured confidence threshold and determines it cannot resolve with sufficient confidence.\n\nData passed at escalation:\n\n- Full conversation transcript.\n\n- Customer identity (name, account, contact record).\n\n- Issue summary (what the AI understood the customer's need to be).\n\n- Actions already taken by the AI (e.g., \"I looked up order #12345 and it shows shipped\").\n\n- Case record if one was created.\n\nThe receiving agent should be able to pick up without asking the customer to repeat themselves.")
                    ]
                )
            ]
        ),

        // Section 15: Explain agentic service capabilities, use cases, and configu (18 topics)
        StudySection(
            id: "s15_explain_agentic_service_capabilities_use",
            title: "Explain agentic service capabilities, use cases, and configuration processes.",
            color: Color(red: 0.25, green: 0.55, blue: 0.70),
            lightColor: Color(red: 0.88, green: 0.95, blue: 0.98),
            icon: "globe",
            objectives: [
                StudyObjective(
                    id: "s15_explain_agentic_service_capabilities_use_obj",
                    title: "Explain agentic service capabilities, use cases, and configuration processes.",
                    topics: [
                        StudyTopic(id: "s15_explain_agentic_service_capabilities_use_1", number: 1, question: "What types of actions an Agentforce Service Agent can perform out of the box (e.g., case creation, knowledge retrieval, order lookups) and what requires custom configuration", answer: "Out-of-the-box actions (standard):\n\n- Create a case.\n\n- Search and retrieve Knowledge articles.\n\n- Look up contact/account information.\n\n- Check case status.\n\n- Transfer/escalate to a human agent.\n\n- Send a messaging session summary.\n\nRequires custom configuration:\n\n- Order status lookups from external ERP (requires Apex action or external API connection).\n\n- Custom eligibility checks (requires Flow or Apex action).\n\n- Processing refunds or cancellations (requires custom action with appropriate guardrails).\n\n- Integrations with non-Salesforce systems (requires custom action definitions).\n\nCustom actions are defined as Agent Actions in the Agentforce Agent Builder and are invoked by the AI when the customer's intent matches."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s15_explain_agentic_service_capabilities_use_2", number: 2, question: "How Agentforce for Service is configured, including topics, actions, and the agent builder interface in Salesforce", answer: "Configuration steps:"),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s15_explain_agentic_service_capabilities_use_3", number: 3, question: "**Agent Builder** (Setup > Agentforce > Agents): the central interface for creating and configuring Agentforce agents.", answer: ""),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s15_explain_agentic_service_capabilities_use_4", number: 4, question: "**Create Agent**: name the agent, select type (Service Agent), configure the base persona and instructions.", answer: ""),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s15_explain_agentic_service_capabilities_use_5", number: 5, question: "**Define Topics**: topics define the areas the agent handles (e.g., \"Billing Inquiries,\" \"Order Status,\" \"Password Reset\"). Each topic has a description that helps the AI determine which topic applies to a customer's message.", answer: ""),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s15_explain_agentic_service_capabilities_use_6", number: 6, question: "**Configure Actions per Topic**: assign standard or custom actions to each topic. Actions are what the AI executes (create case, look up order, retrieve article).", answer: ""),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s15_explain_agentic_service_capabilities_use_7", number: 7, question: "**Guardrails**: define what the agent is NOT allowed to do (e.g., \"Do not provide medical advice,\" \"Always escalate complaints about legal matters\").", answer: ""),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s15_explain_agentic_service_capabilities_use_8", number: 8, question: "**Test**: use the Agent Builder preview to simulate conversations and validate topic and action routing.", answer: ""),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s15_explain_agentic_service_capabilities_use_9", number: 9, question: "**Publish**: deploy to a channel (MIAW, Experience Cloud).", answer: ""),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s15_explain_agentic_service_capabilities_use_10", number: 10, question: "How to configure and publish an Agentforce Service Agent to a Messaging for In-App and Web channel, including channel linking and testing", answer: "Steps:"),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s15_explain_agentic_service_capabilities_use_11", number: 11, question: "Complete Agent Builder configuration (topics, actions, guardrails).", answer: ""),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s15_explain_agentic_service_capabilities_use_12", number: 12, question: "In the Agent Builder, navigate to **Channels** and select the MIAW messaging channel to link the agent to.", answer: ""),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s15_explain_agentic_service_capabilities_use_13", number: 13, question: "In **Messaging Settings** (Setup > Messaging > Messaging Settings), find the target MIAW channel and confirm the Agentforce agent is assigned.", answer: ""),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s15_explain_agentic_service_capabilities_use_14", number: 14, question: "**Test mode**: use the Agent Builder's built-in preview to simulate conversations. Also test via a sandbox Embedded Service Deployment on a test page.", answer: ""),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s15_explain_agentic_service_capabilities_use_15", number: 15, question: "Validate: confirm escalation to human agent works; confirm topic routing is correct for sample utterances; confirm actions execute correctly.", answer: ""),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s15_explain_agentic_service_capabilities_use_16", number: 16, question: "**Activate** the agent in Agent Builder and confirm the MIAW channel is live.", answer: ""),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s15_explain_agentic_service_capabilities_use_17", number: 17, question: "Monitor using Omni Supervisor and Agentforce Analytics in the initial days after launch.", answer: ""),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s15_explain_agentic_service_capabilities_use_18", number: 18, question: "The limitations of Agentforce Service Agents in terms of supported channels, action complexity, and compliance use cases that require human oversight", answer: "Channel limitations:\n\n- Agentforce Service Agents work primarily on text-based channels (MIAW, web chat, SMS). Voice channel support is limited/evolving.\n\n- Not all Experience Cloud templates support embedded Agentforce agents.\n\nAction complexity limitations:\n\n- Actions must be pre-defined; the AI cannot spontaneously execute actions not in its configured set.\n\n- Long multi-step processes with many conditional branches may be better served by Flows than AI agent actions.\n\n- External API latency affects AI agent response time.\n\nCompliance use cases requiring human oversight:\n\n- Regulated industries (healthcare, finance, legal) may prohibit AI from making commitments or providing advice without human review.\n\n- PII handling by AI agents must comply with data residency and processing regulations.\n\n- Audit trail requirements: all AI actions must be logged; Salesforce logs agent actions, but organizations must verify logging meets compliance standards.\n\n- AI agents should never be the final decision-maker in high-stakes scenarios (e.g., loan approvals, medical recommendations).")
                    ]
                )
            ]
        ),

        // Section 16: Given a set of requirements or KPIs, design a case managemen (25 topics)
        StudySection(
            id: "s16_given_a_set_of_requirements_or_kpis_desi",
            title: "Given a set of requirements or KPIs, design a case management solution from creation to closure.",
            color: Color(red: 0.70, green: 0.30, blue: 0.40),
            lightColor: Color(red: 0.98, green: 0.90, blue: 0.92),
            icon: "person.2.fill",
            objectives: [
                StudyObjective(
                    id: "s16_given_a_set_of_requirements_or_kpis_desi_obj",
                    title: "Given a set of requirements or KPIs, design a case management solution from creation to closure.",
                    topics: [
                        StudyTopic(id: "s16_given_a_set_of_requirements_or_kpis_desi_1", number: 1, question: "The role of case statuses in lifecycle management and how to design a status picklist that supports KPI reporting without creating agent confusion", answer: "Case statuses define where a case is in its lifecycle and are critical for measuring resolution time, SLA compliance, and agent productivity.\n\nDesign principles:\n\n- Each status should have a clear, unambiguous meaning that agents understand.\n\n- Status values should map to KPIs: \"Waiting on Customer\" pauses SLA clock; \"In Progress\" keeps it running.\n\n- Avoid too many statuses — 5–8 well-defined values is typically sufficient. More causes agent confusion and inconsistent usage.\n\n- Reserve \"Closed\" as a terminal status that triggers automation (surveys, case close template).\n\nExample well-designed status set:\n\nNew → In Progress → Waiting on Customer → Waiting on Engineering → Resolved → Closed\n\nInclude \"Closed\" as distinct from \"Resolved\" if post-resolution verification is part of the SLA."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s16_given_a_set_of_requirements_or_kpis_desi_2", number: 2, question: "How to design assignment rules that route cases to the correct queue based on case origin, product, priority, and customer tier", answer: "Assignment Rules evaluate criteria top-to-bottom and apply the first matching rule:"),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s16_given_a_set_of_requirements_or_kpis_desi_3", number: 3, question: "Setup > Case Assignment Rules > New Rule.", answer: ""),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s16_given_a_set_of_requirements_or_kpis_desi_4", number: 4, question: "Add Rule Entries with criteria and an assigned queue or user.", answer: "Example rule entries (in priority order):"),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s16_given_a_set_of_requirements_or_kpis_desi_5", number: 5, question: "Case Origin = \"Phone\" AND Priority = \"Critical\" → P1 Critical Support Queue.", answer: ""),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s16_given_a_set_of_requirements_or_kpis_desi_6", number: 6, question: "Product = \"Hardware\" → Hardware Support Queue.", answer: ""),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s16_given_a_set_of_requirements_or_kpis_desi_7", number: 7, question: "Account: Customer Tier = \"Enterprise\" → Enterprise Support Queue.", answer: ""),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s16_given_a_set_of_requirements_or_kpis_desi_8", number: 8, question: "Default (catch-all) → General Support Queue.", answer: "Best practices:\n\n- Order rules from most specific to least specific.\n\n- Every case should match at least the catch-all rule.\n\n- Test rules in a sandbox with cases that represent all expected variations.\n\n- Use formula criteria for complex conditions that basic field picklists cannot handle."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s16_given_a_set_of_requirements_or_kpis_desi_9", number: 9, question: "How to use case queues, assignment rules, and Omni-Channel together to create an end-to-end routing architecture for a multi-tier support model", answer: "End-to-end architecture:"),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s16_given_a_set_of_requirements_or_kpis_desi_10", number: 10, question: "**Case creation** (Email-to-Case, Web-to-Case, manual): case is created.", answer: ""),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s16_given_a_set_of_requirements_or_kpis_desi_11", number: 11, question: "**Assignment Rule** fires immediately: evaluates criteria (product, priority, origin) and assigns the case to the appropriate **Queue**.", answer: ""),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s16_given_a_set_of_requirements_or_kpis_desi_12", number: 12, question: "**Omni-Channel** detects a new case in the queue: the work item is pushed to an available, appropriately configured agent who is a member of that queue.", answer: ""),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s16_given_a_set_of_requirements_or_kpis_desi_13", number: 13, question: "**Agent accepts** the work item via the Omni-Channel widget: the case is assigned to the agent; they begin working.", answer: ""),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s16_given_a_set_of_requirements_or_kpis_desi_14", number: 14, question: "**Escalation rules**: if the case is not updated within a defined period, it is escalated to a higher-tier queue and Omni-Channel re-routes.", answer: "Queues serve as the staging area between assignment rules and Omni-Channel routing. This architecture cleanly separates routing logic (assignment rules) from agent distribution (Omni-Channel)."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s16_given_a_set_of_requirements_or_kpis_desi_15", number: 15, question: "How email threading in Email-to-Case works so that customer replies are appended to the existing case rather than creating duplicate cases, and what can break this behavior", answer: "Threading mechanism: when Salesforce sends an email from a case, it includes a unique thread ID in the email body (typically in the footer or headers). When the customer replies, the reply contains that thread ID, which Salesforce uses to match the email to the existing case.\n\nWhat breaks threading:\n\n- Customer modifies the email subject line (removes the case number from the subject).\n\n- Customer emails a new address instead of replying (no thread ID in the new email).\n\n- Customer uses a different email client that strips footer content.\n\n- The thread ID is deleted from the email body by the customer.\n\n- Email is forwarded rather than replied to.\n\nBest practice: include the thread ID in multiple locations (subject line with case number AND footer) to maximize threading reliability."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s16_given_a_set_of_requirements_or_kpis_desi_16", number: 16, question: "The use cases for case escalation rules versus workflow-based escalation, including which is more appropriate when time-sensitive SLA thresholds must be enforced", answer: "| | Case Escalation Rules | Workflow/Flow-based Escalation |\n\n|---|---|---|\n\n| Best for | Time-based SLA escalation (case not updated in X hours) | Field-change-based escalation (Priority changed to Critical → notify manager) |\n\n| Trigger | Time elapsed since case creation or last modification | Record save or field change |\n\n| Actions | Reassign case, send email alert | More flexible: field update, email, Chatter post, external callout |\n\n| SLA enforcement | Best fit — designed for time-based thresholds | Not ideal for time-based rules (requires scheduled flows) |\n\nRecommendation: use Case Escalation Rules for time-sensitive SLA thresholds (e.g., \"Escalate if Priority = High and no update in 2 hours\"). Use Flow-based automation for event-driven escalation (e.g., \"When customer replies and case status is Closed, re-open case and notify supervisor\")."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s16_given_a_set_of_requirements_or_kpis_desi_17", number: 17, question: "How to configure case teams to support multi-agent collaboration on a single case, and what limitations exist with case team roles", answer: "Case Teams allow multiple agents (with different roles) to collaborate on a single case:"),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s16_given_a_set_of_requirements_or_kpis_desi_18", number: 18, question: "Setup > Case Team Roles: define roles (e.g., \"Tier 2 Specialist,\" \"Account Manager,\" \"Quality Reviewer\") with read or read/write access.", answer: ""),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s16_given_a_set_of_requirements_or_kpis_desi_19", number: 19, question: "Add team members to a case via the Case Team related list.", answer: ""),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s16_given_a_set_of_requirements_or_kpis_desi_20", number: 20, question: "Use **Predefined Case Teams** (templates) for common team configurations to speed up team setup.", answer: "Access: each team member's role controls their case access level (read vs. read/write).\n\nLimitations:\n\n- Case team roles do not replace case ownership — there is still one case owner.\n\n- Maximum of 5 predefined case teams per org (standard configuration; can be increased).\n\n- Case team members are not automatically notified of case updates unless notification rules are configured.\n\n- Team members cannot be automatically added by automation rules — an agent or admin must add them manually or via Apex/Flow."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s16_given_a_set_of_requirements_or_kpis_desi_21", number: 21, question: "How parent-child case relationships work in Salesforce and when splitting a complex issue into child cases is preferable to managing it as a single record", answer: "Parent-Child Cases: a parent case (container/master) can have multiple child cases linked to it. The parent represents the overall issue; children represent component tasks or sub-issues assigned to different teams.\n\nWhen to use child cases:\n\n- A complex issue requires parallel work by multiple teams (e.g., a product defect involving Engineering, QA, and Documentation).\n\n- Different components of the issue have different SLAs or owners.\n\n- You need separate tracking of resolution for each sub-issue while maintaining a single customer-facing parent case.\n\nHow it works: the parent case can be configured to auto-close when all child cases are closed (via Flow). The customer-facing communication occurs on the parent; internal tracking occurs on children.\n\nLimitation: Salesforce supports only one level of parent-child nesting (children cannot have their own child cases)."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s16_given_a_set_of_requirements_or_kpis_desi_22", number: 22, question: "How to associate multiple contacts with a single case using case contact relationships, and the limitations of the standard case contact field versus a custom relationship", answer: "Standard Case Contact field: a single lookup to one Contact. By default, a case can only be linked to one contact at a time.\n\nMultiple contacts: use the Case Contact Role or a custom junction object to link multiple contacts to a single case.\n\n- Enable Case Contact Roles (if available in your org) to create multiple contact associations with role labels (e.g., \"Primary Contact,\" \"CC'd Contact\").\n\n- Alternatively, create a custom junction object (CaseContact__c) with lookups to Case and Contact for full flexibility.\n\nLimitations of the standard field:\n\n- Only one contact can be linked natively.\n\n- Email notifications go only to the case's primary contact.\n\n- Customer-facing portal visibility is limited to the primary contact's profile.\n\nFor multi-stakeholder cases (e.g., enterprise accounts with multiple contacts involved), a custom relationship is necessary."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s16_given_a_set_of_requirements_or_kpis_desi_23", number: 23, question: "How to configure case close templates and required closure fields to ensure data completeness for reporting and quality analysis", answer: "- Case Close Layout: Setup > Cases > Case Close Page Layouts. Configure a specific page layout for the case close action with required fields.\n\n- Required closure fields: commonly required fields include: Resolution Summary, Root Cause, Close Reason, Time Spent, Knowledge Article (Was an article attached?).\n\n- Validation Rules: add validation rules that fire only when Status = \"Closed\" to enforce that required fields are populated before closure is allowed.\n\n- Close Case Quick Action: configure a Quick Action on the Case object for \"Close Case\" that presents the close layout as a modal — agents complete required fields without navigating away.\n\nData completeness at closure is critical for FCR reporting, root cause analysis, and Knowledge article creation."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s16_given_a_set_of_requirements_or_kpis_desi_24", number: 24, question: "How the case merge feature works, its limitations, and when a consultant should recommend merging duplicate cases versus linking them", answer: "Case Merge: Salesforce allows merging up to 3 duplicate cases. The user selects a master case; related records (emails, comments, attachments) from the non-master cases are merged into the master.\n\nLimitations:\n\n- Maximum 3 cases per merge operation.\n\n- The merge is irreversible — the non-master cases are deleted.\n\n- Merge is done manually (no automated duplicate detection for cases, unlike Contacts/Leads).\n\n- Only available in Lightning Experience (some limitations in Classic).\n\nMerge vs. Link:\n\n- Merge: when cases are true duplicates (same customer, same issue, created by different channels). Eliminates redundancy.\n\n- Link (Parent-Child): when cases are related but distinct issues that need separate tracking. Preserves both records.\n\nRecommend merging when the customer contacted support through two channels for the exact same issue. Recommend linking when issues are related but require separate resolution tracking."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s16_given_a_set_of_requirements_or_kpis_desi_25", number: 25, question: "The difference between public and private case comments, when each is visible to customers in a self-service portal, and how to control comment visibility", answer: "- Public comments: visible to customers in an Experience Cloud self-service portal. Use for customer-facing updates, resolution steps, and responses the customer should see.\n\n- Private comments: visible only to internal Salesforce users (agents, supervisors). Use for internal notes, escalation details, or information not suitable for customer visibility.\n\nControl mechanism:\n\n- On the Case Comment record, the \"Published\" checkbox controls whether the comment is public. When checked, the comment appears in the Experience Cloud portal for the case owner contact.\n\n- Agents must consciously check \"Published\" to make a comment visible externally — the default can be configured.\n\nBest practice: make the default for new comments \"Private\" (unchecked) to prevent accidental exposure of internal notes to customers.")
                    ]
                )
            ]
        ),

        // Section 17: Given a set of requirements, explain how case deflection str (7 topics)
        StudySection(
            id: "s17_given_a_set_of_requirements_explain_how_",
            title: "Given a set of requirements, explain how case deflection strategies are delivered in Experience Cloud sites, Agentforce, and Knowledge.",
            color: Color(red: 0.40, green: 0.60, blue: 0.30),
            lightColor: Color(red: 0.92, green: 0.96, blue: 0.90),
            icon: "leaf.fill",
            objectives: [
                StudyObjective(
                    id: "s17_given_a_set_of_requirements_explain_how__obj",
                    title: "Given a set of requirements, explain how case deflection strategies are delivered in Experience Cloud sites, Agentforce, and Knowledge.",
                    topics: [
                        StudyTopic(id: "s17_given_a_set_of_requirements_explain_how__1", number: 1, question: "How to configure a self-service Experience Cloud site so that customers who search for help see Knowledge articles before being offered a case creation form", answer: "- Place the Knowledge Search component prominently on the site's Help Center or Support page.\n\n- Position the Case Creation form/button below the fold or behind a \"Contact Us\" link — not on the landing page.\n\n- Enable Article Suggestions: as the customer types into the search bar, surface matching articles in a dropdown before they submit.\n\n- Use the Contact Support Flow: build a Flow that presents Knowledge article results first; only shows the case form if the customer confirms none of the articles solved their issue.\n\n- Configure the Experience Cloud site's navigation to make Knowledge search the default entry point, not the case form."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s17_given_a_set_of_requirements_explain_how__2", number: 2, question: "How Agentforce contributes to case deflection by resolving common requests autonomously, and how to measure deflection rate as a KPI", answer: "How Agentforce deflects:\n\n- An Agentforce Service Agent intercepts the customer before they reach a human agent.\n\n- For resolved interactions (customer confirms issue resolved), no case is created.\n\n- Each autonomously resolved interaction is a deflected contact.\n\nMeasuring deflection rate:\n\n- Track Messaging Sessions where the conversation ended without escalating to a human agent AND without a case being created.\n\n- Deflection Rate = (Autonomously resolved sessions / Total AI-initiated sessions) x 100.\n\n- Compare inbound case volume before and after Agentforce deployment to measure overall impact.\n\n- Use Agentforce Analytics (in the Agent Builder) to see resolution vs. escalation ratios."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s17_given_a_set_of_requirements_explain_how__3", number: 3, question: "When a company wants to reduce inbound case volume by 20%, which combination of Knowledge, Experience Cloud, and Agentforce features should be recommended and why", answer: "Recommended combination:"),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s17_given_a_set_of_requirements_explain_how__4", number: 4, question: "**Knowledge**: build a comprehensive, well-categorized article library covering the top 20 issue types (Pareto analysis of existing cases). Surface articles via search and AI suggestions.", answer: ""),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s17_given_a_set_of_requirements_explain_how__5", number: 5, question: "**Experience Cloud**: configure a deflection-first self-service portal (Knowledge search before case form, article suggestions, community Q&A).", answer: ""),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s17_given_a_set_of_requirements_explain_how__6", number: 6, question: "**Agentforce**: deploy a Service Agent on the portal and messaging channels to intercept tier-1 requests (order status, password reset, FAQ). Route to human only when unresolved.", answer: "Why this combination: each layer catches different customer segments. Self-serve readers deflect via Knowledge. Conversational customers deflect via Agentforce. Community participants deflect each other. The 20% target is achievable when all three are active simultaneously."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s17_given_a_set_of_requirements_explain_how__7", number: 7, question: "How to track and report on case deflection events from a self-service portal to demonstrate ROI of the deflection investment", answer: "Tracking mechanisms:\n\n- Knowledge Article views in Experience Cloud: tracked in KnowledgeArticleViewStat.\n\n- Search with no resulting case: requires custom tracking (e.g., a custom object logging searches that did not lead to case creation, or a Flow that captures article view without subsequent case creation).\n\n- Agentforce resolved sessions: tracked in MessagingSession with resolution outcome.\n\n- Community answers: Q&A items resolved without case creation.\n\nReporting:\n\n- Build a dashboard showing: article views per month, cases NOT created per month (estimated deflection), and cost savings (deflected contacts x cost-per-contact).\n\n- Compare inbound case volume trend before and after each deflection channel was launched.\n\nROI = (Deflected contacts x cost-per-contact) / Investment in deflection tools.")
                    ]
                )
            ]
        ),

        // Section 18: Explain the capabilities, use cases, and how to configure se (16 topics)
        StudySection(
            id: "s18_explain_the_capabilities_use_cases_and_h",
            title: "Explain the capabilities, use cases, and how to configure service entitlements, milestones, assets, business hours, and service-level agreements (...)",
            color: Color(red: 0.50, green: 0.30, blue: 0.60),
            lightColor: Color(red: 0.94, green: 0.90, blue: 0.96),
            icon: "cpu.fill",
            objectives: [
                StudyObjective(
                    id: "s18_explain_the_capabilities_use_cases_and_h_obj",
                    title: "Explain the capabilities, use cases, and how to configure service entitlements, milestones, assets, business hours, and service-level agreements (...)",
                    topics: [
                        StudyTopic(id: "s18_explain_the_capabilities_use_cases_and_h_1", number: 1, question: "The difference between a Service Contract, an Entitlement, and an Entitlement Process in Salesforce", answer: "- Service Contract: a formal agreement between the company and a customer defining the terms of support (duration, coverage, price). A service contract is the parent record.\n\n- Entitlement: the specific support right granted to a customer (e.g., \"24/7 phone support,\" \"Email support, 9–5\"). One service contract can have multiple entitlements. Entitlements are linked to cases to verify the customer is eligible for support.\n\n- Entitlement Process: the set of time-dependent milestones and actions that define the SLA workflow for cases associated with an entitlement (e.g., \"Respond within 4 hours, Resolve within 24 hours\").\n\nAnalogy: the Service Contract is the overall agreement; the Entitlement is what the customer is entitled to; the Entitlement Process defines how that entitlement is measured and enforced."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s18_explain_the_capabilities_use_cases_and_h_2", number: 2, question: "When a Service Contract, Entitlement, or Entitlement Process is used to model a customer's support agreement", answer: "- Service Contract: use when you need to track the commercial/contractual agreement (start date, end date, pricing, terms). Required for companies that sell support contracts separately from product.\n\n- Entitlement: use to verify that a customer is eligible to receive support before opening a case. Prevents unauthorized support consumption.\n\n- Entitlement Process: use when you need to enforce time-based SLA milestones with automated alerts and escalations. Required for SLA compliance tracking and reporting.\n\nAll three can be used together or independently. A minimal SLA implementation may use only Entitlements + Entitlement Process without Service Contracts."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s18_explain_the_capabilities_use_cases_and_h_3", number: 3, question: "How entitlements work in Service Cloud and how they enforce SLA policies on cases", answer: ""),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s18_explain_the_capabilities_use_cases_and_h_4", number: 4, question: "An Entitlement record is created for a contact/account (linked to a Service Contract if used).", answer: ""),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s18_explain_the_capabilities_use_cases_and_h_5", number: 5, question: "When a case is created for that contact/account, the entitlement is automatically associated with the case (via entitlement lookup or assignment rules).", answer: ""),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s18_explain_the_capabilities_use_cases_and_h_6", number: 6, question: "The entitlement tells the agent: \"This customer is entitled to 24/7 support with a 4-hour response SLA.\"", answer: ""),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s18_explain_the_capabilities_use_cases_and_h_7", number: 7, question: "The associated Entitlement Process starts the milestone clock when the case is created/entitlement applied.", answer: ""),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s18_explain_the_capabilities_use_cases_and_h_8", number: 8, question: "If a milestone is not met within the defined time, a violation action fires (email alert, field update, Chatter notification).", answer: "Entitlements can be configured to require verification: agents see a visual indicator (green/red) on the case showing whether the customer has an active entitlement."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s18_explain_the_capabilities_use_cases_and_h_9", number: 9, question: "The difference between entitlement processes and milestones in Service Cloud", answer: "- Entitlement Process: the overall SLA framework — defines the sequence of milestones, business hours, and rules for a class of cases. Think of it as the SLA \"template.\"\n\n- Milestone: a specific time-based checkpoint within the entitlement process (e.g., \"First Response within 1 hour,\" \"Case Resolution within 8 hours\"). Each milestone has success criteria (when it is marked complete), warning actions (approaching breach), and violation actions (breach occurred).\n\nMultiple milestones can exist within one Entitlement Process. Each case gets its own CaseMilestone records (instances of the milestones) when the entitlement process applies."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s18_explain_the_capabilities_use_cases_and_h_10", number: 10, question: "How business hours are configured in Salesforce and how they affect milestone time calculations when a case is open across a weekend or holiday", answer: "Configuration:"),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s18_explain_the_capabilities_use_cases_and_h_11", number: 11, question: "Setup > Business Hours > New: define name, time zone, and operating hours for each day of the week.", answer: ""),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s18_explain_the_capabilities_use_cases_and_h_12", number: 12, question: "Mark specific dates as holidays (Setup > Holidays > link to Business Hours record).", answer: ""),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s18_explain_the_capabilities_use_cases_and_h_13", number: 13, question: "Associate Business Hours with the Entitlement Process.", answer: "Effect on milestone calculations:\n\n- Milestone timers only count time within configured business hours.\n\n- If a case is opened Friday at 4:45 PM and the first response SLA is 1 business hour, the timer pauses at 5 PM Friday and resumes Monday at 9 AM.\n\n- Without business hours configured, the 1-hour timer would fire at 5:45 PM Friday (during off-hours), creating false SLA breaches."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s18_explain_the_capabilities_use_cases_and_h_14", number: 14, question: "What milestone completion criteria and actions are available in an entitlement process, and how violation actions trigger alerts or escalations", answer: "Completion criteria: a formula or field value that marks the milestone as complete. Example: First Response milestone = complete when a public case comment or email is sent to the customer.\n\nActions available:\n\n- Success actions: triggered when the milestone is completed on time (e.g., log a task, send a congratulatory notification).\n\n- Warning actions: triggered X minutes before the milestone deadline (e.g., email alert to the agent warning of approaching breach).\n\n- Violation actions: triggered when the milestone deadline passes without completion (e.g., email alert to manager, reassign case to escalation queue, field update to set Priority = \"Critical\").\n\nViolation actions are critical for SLA enforcement — they provide the automated escalation that prevents cases from silently breaching SLAs."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s18_explain_the_capabilities_use_cases_and_h_15", number: 15, question: "When a company has different SLA tiers for different customer contracts, how to configure multiple entitlement processes with different milestone timelines", answer: "- Create one Entitlement Process per SLA tier (e.g., \"Gold SLA Process,\" \"Silver SLA Process,\" \"Bronze SLA Process\").\n\n- Each process has its own milestones with different timelines (Gold: 1-hour response, 4-hour resolution; Silver: 4-hour response, 24-hour resolution; Bronze: 24-hour response, 72-hour resolution).\n\n- Create Entitlements for each customer/contract linked to the appropriate Entitlement Process.\n\n- When a case is created and associated with the customer's entitlement, the correct Entitlement Process applies automatically.\n\nThis allows a single Salesforce org to enforce different SLAs for different customer tiers without manual intervention."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s18_explain_the_capabilities_use_cases_and_h_16", number: 16, question: "How assets are related to cases and entitlements, and the use case for tracking product-specific support history through the asset object", answer: "Asset: represents a specific instance of a product that a customer owns (serial number, purchase date, warranty expiry, installed location).\n\nRelationship to cases: a case can be linked to an Asset, indicating which specific product the support issue relates to.\n\nRelationship to entitlements: entitlements can be linked to an Asset (instead of just an Account or Contact), meaning the support agreement is for a specific product instance. This is useful when support terms vary by product (e.g., a hardware warranty vs. a software subscription).\n\nUse case for tracking support history: by linking cases to Assets, you can report on all cases associated with a specific product unit over time — identifying high-defect units, tracking warranty usage, and informing product engineering of recurring field issues.")
                    ]
                )
            ]
        ),

        // Section 19: Differentiate the use cases and capabilities of Service Clou (11 topics)
        StudySection(
            id: "s19_differentiate_the_use_cases_and_capabili",
            title: "Differentiate the use cases and capabilities of Service Cloud automation.",
            color: Color(red: 0.60, green: 0.50, blue: 0.20),
            lightColor: Color(red: 0.98, green: 0.96, blue: 0.88),
            icon: "lightbulb.fill",
            objectives: [
                StudyObjective(
                    id: "s19_differentiate_the_use_cases_and_capabili_obj",
                    title: "Differentiate the use cases and capabilities of Service Cloud automation.",
                    topics: [
                        StudyTopic(id: "s19_differentiate_the_use_cases_and_capabili_1", number: 1, question: "When to use case assignment rules versus escalation rules, and how they differ or overlap in functionality", answer: "| | Case Assignment Rules | Case Escalation Rules |\n\n|---|---|---|\n\n| Purpose | Route a case to the correct owner/queue at creation or re-assignment | Escalate a case that has not been updated within a time threshold |\n\n| Trigger | Case creation or edit (when \"Assign using active assignment rules\" is checked) | Time elapsed since case creation or last modification |\n\n| Action | Change case owner to user or queue | Reassign owner, send email alert |\n\n| Use case | Initial routing: \"Route Hardware cases to Hardware Queue\" | SLA enforcement: \"Escalate High priority cases not updated in 2 hours\" |\n\nOverlap: both rules can reassign a case. The key differentiator is time-trigger (escalation) vs. event-trigger (assignment)."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s19_differentiate_the_use_cases_and_capabili_2", number: 2, question: "When to use auto-response rules versus workflow rules or flows, and how they differ or overlap in functionality", answer: "| | Auto-Response Rules | Workflow Rules / Flows |\n\n|---|---|---|\n\n| Purpose | Send an acknowledgment email when a case is created via Email-to-Case or Web-to-Case | Automate a broad range of actions on any case event |\n\n| Trigger | Case creation only | Any case field change, creation, scheduled time |\n\n| Actions | Email only | Email, field update, task creation, Chatter post, call Apex, call external action |\n\n| Best for | Immediate customer acknowledgment on case creation | Complex conditional logic, multi-step automation, scheduled actions |\n\nRecommendation: use Auto-Response Rules only for case creation acknowledgments. Use Flows for everything else — they are more powerful, more maintainable, and Salesforce's strategic automation tool."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s19_differentiate_the_use_cases_and_capabili_3", number: 3, question: "The use case for Screen Flows launched from a case record versus background automation using Record-Triggered Flows in a Service Cloud context", answer: "Screen Flows (agent-facing):\n\n- Launched by an agent via a Quick Action or button on the case record.\n\n- Present a guided, step-by-step UI to the agent.\n\n- Use case: case closure guided interview (\"What was the root cause? Was the issue resolved? Attach a Knowledge article.\").\n\n- Also used for agent-guided troubleshooting scripts.\n\n- Agents control when the flow runs.\n\nRecord-Triggered Flows (background):\n\n- Run automatically when a case record meets defined criteria (on create, on update).\n\n- No UI — run in the background without agent interaction.\n\n- Use case: when a case status changes to \"Resolved,\" automatically send a survey invitation, update a custom metric field, and create a follow-up task.\n\n- Agents are unaware of these automations unless they see field changes."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s19_differentiate_the_use_cases_and_capabili_4", number: 4, question: "How macros in the Service Console differ from Quick Actions and when each is the more appropriate automation tool for agent productivity", answer: "| | Macros | Quick Actions |\n\n|---|---|---|\n\n| What they do | Execute a sequence of multiple actions (apply email template, update status, add comment) in the console with one click | Launch a pre-configured action form (create record, update field, send email) with one click |\n\n| Where they run | Inside the Service Console only (utility bar or Macros component) | On any record page (standard or console) |\n\n| Configuration | Macros Builder (chains console actions) | Quick Action setup in Object Manager |\n\n| Best for | Multi-step repetitive console workflows (close case + send email + log activity in one click) | Single-step shortcuts (create a task, update priority, send a quick email) |\n\nUse macros for complex, multi-step console workflows. Use Quick Actions for simple, single-step shortcuts available on any page."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s19_differentiate_the_use_cases_and_capabili_5", number: 5, question: "How Einstein Case Classification uses AI to automatically populate case fields at creation, and what training data and configuration is required", answer: "Einstein Case Classification uses a machine learning model trained on historical cases to predict field values for new cases:\n\n- When a new case is created (typically via Email-to-Case or Web-to-Case), it analyzes the subject and description and predicts field values (Case Type, Priority, Product, Reason).\n\n- Predictions are applied as field suggestions or auto-populated values, reducing agent effort.\n\nTraining data requirements:\n\n- Minimum ~1,000 closed cases with field values populated on the fields to be classified.\n\n- The model trains on historical case data; field values must be consistently applied in historical data for predictions to be accurate.\n\nConfiguration:"),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s19_differentiate_the_use_cases_and_capabili_6", number: 6, question: "Setup > Einstein > Einstein Classification Apps > Case Classification.", answer: ""),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s19_differentiate_the_use_cases_and_capabili_7", number: 7, question: "Select which fields to classify.", answer: ""),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s19_differentiate_the_use_cases_and_capabili_8", number: 8, question: "Set confidence threshold: below threshold = suggest to agent; above threshold = auto-apply.", answer: ""),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s19_differentiate_the_use_cases_and_capabili_9", number: 9, question: "Activate the app; the model trains on existing data (may take 24–48 hours).", answer: ""),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s19_differentiate_the_use_cases_and_capabili_10", number: 10, question: "When a company wants to automatically update case fields when a related contact record is updated, which automation tool is most appropriate and why", answer: "Most appropriate tool: Record-Triggered Flow on the Contact object.\n\n- Trigger: Contact record is updated (on update).\n\n- Criteria: define which field change is relevant (e.g., Contact.Region changes).\n\n- Action: use a Get Records element to find related open cases, then a Loop + Update Records to update the case field.\n\nWhy not workflow rules: Workflow Rules cannot update related records (child/parent relationships) — they can only update the record they are triggered on.\n\nWhy not Process Builder: Process Builder is deprecated in newer orgs; Flows are Salesforce's current recommended tool.\n\nGovernor limit consideration: if a contact has thousands of related cases, the Flow must use collections (not single record updates in a loop) to stay within DML limits."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s19_differentiate_the_use_cases_and_capabili_11", number: 11, question: "How email alert workflow actions differ from Case Comment notifications, and when each is the appropriate mechanism for keeping stakeholders informed", answer: "| | Email Alert (Workflow/Flow Action) | Case Comment Notification |\n\n|---|---|---|\n\n| Trigger | Any workflow rule or flow condition | Case comment is added (public or private) |\n\n| Recipients | Any user, email address, or contact | Case owner, team members, customer (if public comment on portal) |\n\n| Content | Custom email template with merge fields | Standard notification that a comment was added; links to case |\n\n| Configuration | Email Alert action in workflow/flow | Case Feed notification settings and Experience Cloud notification rules |\n\n| Best for | Proactive stakeholder notifications on field changes, SLA events, or case status changes | Conversational notifications when a case comment is added |\n\nUse Email Alerts for proactive, event-driven notifications (e.g., \"SLA breach imminent\" or \"Priority escalated\"). Use Case Comment notifications for conversational updates on the case feed.")
                    ]
                )
            ]
        ),

        // Section 20: Given a scenario, develop reports and dashboards in Salesfor (22 topics)
        StudySection(
            id: "s20_given_a_scenario_develop_reports_and_das",
            title: "Given a scenario, develop reports and dashboards in Salesforce to provide relevant information to different stakeholders.",
            color: Color(red: 0.35, green: 0.45, blue: 0.65),
            lightColor: Color(red: 0.90, green: 0.92, blue: 0.96),
            icon: "building.2.fill",
            objectives: [
                StudyObjective(
                    id: "s20_given_a_scenario_develop_reports_and_das_obj",
                    title: "Given a scenario, develop reports and dashboards in Salesforce to provide relevant information to different stakeholders.",
                    topics: [
                        StudyTopic(id: "s20_given_a_scenario_develop_reports_and_das_1", number: 1, question: "The difference between summary, matrix, and joined report types in Salesforce", answer: "| Report Type | Structure | Best For |\n\n|---|---|---|\n\n| Summary | Rows grouped by one or more fields; subtotals per group | Grouped counts/totals (cases by queue, by agent) |\n\n| Matrix | Rows AND columns grouped; creates a grid of aggregated values | Two-dimensional analysis (cases by queue AND by month) |\n\n| Joined | Multiple report blocks from different report types combined in one view | Cross-object analysis in a single report (e.g., Cases + Knowledge Articles side by side) |\n\nTabular reports (the fourth type) are simple row-by-row lists without grouping — useful for exports but limited for analytics."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s20_given_a_scenario_develop_reports_and_das_2", number: 2, question: "When a joined report is necessary for contact center analytics across multiple objects", answer: "A Joined Report is necessary when you need to display data from more than one report type side by side in a single report. Examples in a contact center context:\n\n- Compare open cases by queue alongside Knowledge article view counts (Cases block + Knowledge Articles block).\n\n- Show FCR metrics from Cases alongside agent training completion from a custom object.\n\n- Display SLA compliance from CaseMilestone alongside customer satisfaction from Survey Response.\n\nStandard reports only support one primary object and its related objects. Joined reports bridge unrelated objects in one view."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s20_given_a_scenario_develop_reports_and_das_3", number: 3, question: "When an executive stakeholder wants a high-level view of contact center health, which standard Salesforce report types and dashboard components are most appropriate", answer: "Report types:\n\n- Cases (for volume, status, SLA compliance).\n\n- CaseMilestone (for SLA breach rate).\n\n- AgentWork (Omni-Channel, for handle time and routing efficiency).\n\n- Survey Response (for CSAT).\n\nDashboard components:\n\n- Metric component: single number (total open cases, CSAT score). High impact, easy to read.\n\n- Gauge component: target vs. actual (SLA compliance %).\n\n- Bar/Column chart: cases by channel, by queue, by priority.\n\n- Line chart: volume trend over time (month-over-month).\n\nKeep executive dashboards to 5–7 components maximum. Focus on outcome metrics (CSAT, SLA compliance, FCR) rather than operational details."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s20_given_a_scenario_develop_reports_and_das_4", number: 4, question: "How to design a case management dashboard for a contact center manager that shows open case volume, SLA compliance, and average handle time by queue", answer: "Dashboard design:\n\n- Component 1 — Open Case Volume by Queue: bar chart; Cases report grouped by Queue.\n\n- Component 2 — SLA Compliance Rate: gauge or metric; CaseMilestone report showing % milestones completed on time.\n\n- Component 3 — Average Handle Time by Queue: metric or bar chart; AgentWork report grouped by Queue with average handle time.\n\n- Component 4 — Cases by Age (Aging Report): horizontal bar chart; cases bucketed by days open.\n\n- Component 5 — Escalated Cases: metric showing count of cases with Priority = \"Critical\" or Status = \"Escalated.\"\n\nFilter: use a dashboard-level date filter (this week, this month). Refresh: schedule daily refresh."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s20_given_a_scenario_develop_reports_and_das_5", number: 5, question: "How to design role-appropriate report access using folder sharing and public group assignments in Salesforce to ensure the right stakeholders see the right data", answer: "Mechanism: Report and Dashboard Folders + Sharing settings.\n\n- Create separate folders for each stakeholder group: \"Agent Reports,\" \"Manager Reports,\" \"Executive Reports.\"\n\n- Share each folder with the appropriate Public Group (e.g., the \"Contact Center Managers\" Public Group sees the Manager Reports folder).\n\n- Set folder access level: Viewer (run reports), Editor (modify reports), or Manager (manage folder).\n\n- Do NOT place all reports in a single shared folder — this exposes sensitive data (e.g., individual agent performance) to all users.\n\nDynamic Dashboards (see topic 6) provide an additional layer: each user sees their own data when they open the dashboard, without the need for separate dashboards per user."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s20_given_a_scenario_develop_reports_and_das_6", number: 6, question: "How dynamic dashboards work in Salesforce and when they are the appropriate choice over standard dashboards for showing individual agent-level metrics to each agent", answer: "Dynamic Dashboards: run as the logged-in user rather than a fixed user. Each agent who opens the dashboard sees data filtered to their own cases, their own handle time, their own SLA metrics.\n\nConfiguration: when creating a dashboard, set \"Run As\" = \"The dashboard viewer\" (instead of a specific user).\n\nWhen to use dynamic dashboards:\n\n- You want each agent to see only their own performance metrics.\n\n- You want supervisors and agents to see the same dashboard but with different scopes (supervisor sees all; agent sees self).\n\n- Avoids the need to create 50 individual dashboards for 50 agents.\n\nLimitation: maximum of 5 dynamic dashboards per org in the standard edition (varies by edition)."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s20_given_a_scenario_develop_reports_and_das_7", number: 7, question: "How to use Omni-Channel reporting objects (AgentWork, UserServicePresence) to report on agent availability, handle time, and routing efficiency", answer: "Key objects:\n\n- AgentWork: one record per work item handled by an agent. Contains: agent, queue, channel, handle time, open time, accept time, close time.\n\n- UserServicePresence: tracks agent presence status over time (Available, Busy, Offline). Contains: agent, status, start time, end time, channel.\n\nReports:\n\n- Handle time by agent: AgentWork report, grouped by Agent, showing average handle time.\n\n- Agent availability: UserServicePresence report showing time in each status per agent.\n\n- Routing efficiency: AgentWork report showing average time from work item creation to agent acceptance (queue wait time).\n\nThese reports require the Omni-Channel feature to be active and the corresponding report types to be enabled in your org."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s20_given_a_scenario_develop_reports_and_das_8", number: 8, question: "When a company needs to track SLA compliance using entitlement milestones in reports, which report objects are required and what limitations exist", answer: "Required report type: Cases with Milestones (uses the CaseMilestone object).\n\nKey fields: Milestone Name, Target Date, Completion Date, Is Completed, Is Violated.\n\nCalculating SLA compliance rate: filter for cases where the milestone is Completed = True AND Completion Date <= Target Date (met) versus Completed = False AND Target Date in the past (violated).\n\nLimitations:\n\n- CaseMilestone reports can only be built using the \"Cases with Milestones\" report type — not from a standard Cases report.\n\n- Archived or closed entitlement processes may not surface milestones correctly in reports.\n\n- Milestone data reflects the state at the time of reporting — it does not retroactively correct for business hours configuration changes.\n\n- Large volumes of CaseMilestone records can slow report performance; use filters."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s20_given_a_scenario_develop_reports_and_das_9", number: 9, question: "How to report on Knowledge article usage and deflection by embedding article view and attach metrics into case reporting", answer: "Reporting on article usage:\n\n- KnowledgeArticleViewStat: tracks views per article per channel (internal, customer, partner).\n\n- Cases with Knowledge Articles: report type that shows which articles are attached to cases (uses the KnowledgeArticleCase junction).\n\n- Combine: build a report showing top-viewed articles + attach rate (articles attached to cases as a % of views).\n\nDeflection reporting:\n\n- Track Experience Cloud article views via KnowledgeArticleViewStat (channel = \"Customer\").\n\n- Compare article view volume to case creation volume over the same period.\n\n- Cases NOT created after an article view require custom tracking (a Flow that logs a \"deflection event\" record).\n\nEmbedding in case reporting: use a Joined Report with a Cases block and a Knowledge Articles block to see side-by-side volume."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s20_given_a_scenario_develop_reports_and_das_10", number: 10, question: "How to build a report that tracks First Contact Resolution (FCR) rate by case type and agent, and what custom fields or process requirements support this metric", answer: "FCR requires a custom field: create a checkbox field on Case called \"First Contact Resolution\" (FCR__c). This must be populated at case closure by the agent or by automation.\n\nAutomation option: a Record-Triggered Flow on case closure checks if there are zero related cases from the same contact in the past X days AND the case was not escalated, then auto-checks the FCR field.\n\nReport design:\n\n- Report type: Cases.\n\n- Grouping: by Case Type (row), by Agent (column) — Matrix report.\n\n- Metrics: Count of cases where FCR = True / Total cases = FCR Rate.\n\n- Use a row-level formula to calculate FCR% per group.\n\nProcess requirement: agents (or automation) must populate the FCR field consistently at closure. Without this discipline, the report is unreliable."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s20_given_a_scenario_develop_reports_and_das_11", number: 11, question: "How to use bucketing and cross-filters in reports to segment case volume by resolution type, priority tier, or channel without creating custom fields", answer: "Bucketing: groups existing field values into custom buckets within the report (no field creation needed).\n\n- Example: bucket Case Priority: \"Critical\" + \"High\" → \"Urgent Tier\"; \"Medium\" + \"Low\" → \"Standard Tier.\"\n\n- Add a bucket column to the report; group or filter by the bucket.\n\nCross-Filters: filter the report to include or exclude records based on a related object's criteria.\n\n- Example: \"Show only Cases that HAVE related case milestones that are violated\" → cross-filter on CaseMilestone where Is Violated = True.\n\n- \"Show only Cases WITHOUT a Knowledge article attached\" → Cases WITHOUT KnowledgeArticleCase records → cross-filter.\n\nBucketing and cross-filters are powerful for ad-hoc segmentation without involving an admin to create new fields."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s20_given_a_scenario_develop_reports_and_das_12", number: 12, question: "How row-level formula fields in reports can calculate derived metrics (e.g., days to resolve, SLA breach percentage) without requiring custom formula fields on the object", answer: "Row-level formulas are calculated columns in a Salesforce report (added via the report builder > Add Formula Column) that compute a value for each row using fields on that row.\n\nExamples:\n\n- Days to resolve: `TODAY() - CreatedDate` (for open cases) or `ClosedDate - CreatedDate` (for closed cases).\n\n- SLA breach indicator: `IF(CaseMilestone.Completion_Date > CaseMilestone.Target_Date, \"Breached\", \"Met\")`.\n\n- Handle time in minutes: `(Case.ClosedDate - Case.CreatedDate) * 1440`.\n\nRow-level formulas exist only within the report — they do not create a new field on the object and do not affect Salesforce storage. They are ideal for quick analytics without schema changes."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s20_given_a_scenario_develop_reports_and_das_13", number: 13, question: "How to configure report subscriptions and dashboard scheduling to deliver automated KPI summaries to team leaders without manual effort", answer: "Report Subscriptions:"),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s20_given_a_scenario_develop_reports_and_das_14", number: 14, question: "On any report, click \"Subscribe.\"", answer: ""),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s20_given_a_scenario_develop_reports_and_das_15", number: 15, question: "Set frequency (daily, weekly, monthly) and time.", answer: ""),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s20_given_a_scenario_develop_reports_and_das_16", number: 16, question: "Set conditions: send only if a field meets a threshold (e.g., open critical cases > 10).", answer: ""),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s20_given_a_scenario_develop_reports_and_das_17", number: 17, question: "Choose recipients (yourself or additional users, up to 5).", answer: ""),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s20_given_a_scenario_develop_reports_and_das_18", number: 18, question: "The recipient receives the report results via email.", answer: "Dashboard Scheduling:"),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s20_given_a_scenario_develop_reports_and_das_19", number: 19, question: "On a dashboard, click the Schedule Refresh or email scheduling option.", answer: ""),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s20_given_a_scenario_develop_reports_and_das_20", number: 20, question: "Set the refresh schedule (hourly, daily, weekly).", answer: ""),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s20_given_a_scenario_develop_reports_and_das_21", number: 21, question: "Configure email delivery of a dashboard snapshot to a distribution list or public group.", answer: "Both features allow team leaders to receive KPI updates without logging in to Salesforce daily, improving executive visibility."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s20_given_a_scenario_develop_reports_and_das_22", number: 22, question: "The limitations of standard Salesforce reporting for contact center analytics and when to recommend CRM Analytics (formerly Tableau CRM/Einstein Analytics) as a supplement", answer: "Standard Salesforce reporting limitations:\n\n- Maximum 2,000 report rows displayed in the UI (can export more).\n\n- Limited to 20 custom summary formulas per report.\n\n- Joined reports support maximum 5 blocks.\n\n- No real-time streaming dashboards.\n\n- Limited ability to blend data from external sources.\n\n- Complex predictive analytics and trend forecasting are not possible natively.\n\n- Historical snapshots (e.g., daily case volume trend) require manual effort or scheduled data capture.\n\nRecommend CRM Analytics when:\n\n- The contact center needs real-time streaming dashboards.\n\n- Data from multiple systems (Salesforce + ERP + telephony) must be blended.\n\n- Predictive analytics (e.g., predicting case volume or agent churn) are required.\n\n- Report volumes regularly exceed standard limits.\n\n- Advanced visualization (heat maps, predictive trend lines) is required.")
                    ]
                )
            ]
        ),

        // Section 21: Explain the use cases and considerations for core Service Cl (7 topics)
        StudySection(
            id: "s21_explain_the_use_cases_and_considerations",
            title: "Explain the use cases and considerations for core Service Cloud integrations, including third-party solutions and connections to external sources of data.",
            color: Color(red: 0.30, green: 0.50, blue: 0.80),
            lightColor: Color(red: 0.90, green: 0.94, blue: 1.00),
            icon: "doc.text.fill",
            objectives: [
                StudyObjective(
                    id: "s21_explain_the_use_cases_and_considerations_obj",
                    title: "Explain the use cases and considerations for core Service Cloud integrations, including third-party solutions and connections to external sources of data.",
                    topics: [
                        StudyTopic(id: "s21_explain_the_use_cases_and_considerations_1", number: 1, question: "The use cases for CTI (Computer Telephony Integration) adapters in Service Cloud and how Open CTI supports third-party telephony providers without native telephony", answer: "CTI adapters connect a third-party telephone system to Salesforce, enabling:\n\n- Screen pop: when a call arrives, the matching contact/case opens automatically.\n\n- Click-to-dial: agents dial from within Salesforce by clicking a phone number.\n\n- Call logging: automatic call log creation linked to the case or contact.\n\n- Presence integration: agent availability in Salesforce reflects phone availability.\n\nOpen CTI: Salesforce's JavaScript API framework that allows third-party telephony vendors to embed their softphone directly in the Service Console utility bar without requiring a desktop plugin. The telephony vendor provides a CTI adapter built on Open CTI; Salesforce does not provide telephony — it provides the integration framework.\n\nKey limitation: Open CTI does not provide call recording, transcription, or real-time AI coaching — those are provided by the telephony vendor's system, not Salesforce."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s21_explain_the_use_cases_and_considerations_2", number: 2, question: "How Service Cloud Voice differs from Open CTI in terms of architecture, native capabilities, and the telephony provider options supported", answer: "| | Open CTI | Service Cloud Voice |\n\n|---|---|---|\n\n| Architecture | Third-party telephony + Salesforce API bridge | Telephony natively embedded in Service Cloud |\n\n| Call transcription | Vendor-dependent | Real-time transcription natively available |\n\n| AI coaching | Vendor-dependent | Einstein Real-Time AI suggestions available during calls |\n\n| Telephony providers | Any provider with an Open CTI adapter | Amazon Connect, Certified Partners, or BYOT |\n\n| Data in Salesforce | Varies by vendor implementation | Full call data stored natively in Salesforce Voice Call object |\n\n| Setup complexity | Varies by vendor | Configurable within Salesforce Setup |\n\nRecommendation: use Service Cloud Voice when you want a native, deeply integrated telephony experience with AI capabilities. Use Open CTI when you already have a telephony vendor with an existing adapter and do not want to replace it."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s21_explain_the_use_cases_and_considerations_3", number: 3, question: "How Salesforce External Objects using Salesforce Connect differ from standard integration approaches, and when external objects are the preferred solution for real-time data display", answer: "Salesforce Connect / External Objects: create Salesforce object definitions that map to external database tables or API endpoints. Data is retrieved in real time (not copied to Salesforce storage) when a user views the record.\n\nDifference from standard integration approaches:\n\n- ETL / Data Loader: copies data into Salesforce. Data can become stale. Storage costs apply.\n\n- Apex callout: custom code fetches data on demand but requires development and maintenance.\n\n- Salesforce Connect: declarative configuration; no code needed; data always current; no Salesforce storage consumed.\n\nWhen external objects are preferred:\n\n- Large external data volumes that would be expensive to store in Salesforce.\n\n- Data that changes frequently and must always be current (e.g., real-time inventory, order status from ERP).\n\n- Read-only display of external data within a Salesforce record page.\n\nLimitation: Salesforce Connect has OData query performance limitations; not suitable for high-frequency, high-volume transactional reads."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s21_explain_the_use_cases_and_considerations_4", number: 4, question: "When a company has customer data in an external ERP and agents need real-time account information while working a case, which integration pattern (external objects, Apex callout, MuleSoft) is appropriate and why", answer: "| Pattern | When to Choose |\n\n|---|---|\n\n| External Objects (Salesforce Connect) | Data is read-only for agents; ERP exposes an OData or custom adapter endpoint; no transformation needed; declarative preferred |\n\n| Apex callout (REST/SOAP) | Custom logic needed to transform ERP data before displaying; moderate volume; development resources available |\n\n| MuleSoft | Complex multi-system integration; bidirectional sync; data transformation and orchestration required; ERP + CRM + other systems involved |\n\nFor a simple \"show me this account's order history from ERP\" use case with a well-structured ERP API, an Apex callout or Salesforce Connect is sufficient. MuleSoft is justified when the integration involves multiple systems, complex business logic, or enterprise-wide reuse of the integration layer."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s21_explain_the_use_cases_and_considerations_5", number: 5, question: "The use case for MuleSoft as an integration middleware for Service Cloud versus direct API integration, and the criteria that justify the added architectural complexity", answer: "MuleSoft is justified when:\n\n- Multiple backend systems need to be integrated (ERP, billing, inventory, shipping) and each has its own API.\n\n- Data transformation and enrichment are required before sending to Salesforce.\n\n- The integration pattern must be reused across multiple applications (not just Service Cloud).\n\n- Bidirectional sync is required with guaranteed delivery and error handling.\n\n- Enterprise governance requires a centralized API management layer.\n\nDirect API integration is sufficient when:\n\n- Only one external system is involved.\n\n- The integration is simple (one object, one direction, minimal transformation).\n\n- Development resources are available and the integration does not need to scale to many systems.\n\nMuleSoft's value is in its Anypoint Platform for API management, reusability, and enterprise-grade reliability — not for simple point-to-point integrations."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s21_explain_the_use_cases_and_considerations_6", number: 6, question: "How to evaluate and recommend AppExchange solutions for common Service Cloud gaps (e.g., advanced telephony, field service, customer surveys) including compatibility and licensing considerations", answer: "Evaluation criteria:\n\n- Compatibility: verify the AppExchange listing specifies compatibility with your Salesforce edition and API version. Check the \"Works with\" section.\n\n- Reviews and ratings: review the AppExchange listing's customer reviews and ratings. Check the number of reviews and recency.\n\n- Managed vs. unmanaged package: managed packages are supported by the vendor; unmanaged packages are open source (no vendor support).\n\n- Licensing: understand per-user, per-org, or consumption-based pricing. Confirm Salesforce license requirements (some AppExchange apps require additional Salesforce licenses).\n\n- Security review: all AppExchange apps must pass Salesforce's security review — verify the review is current.\n\n- Vendor support: evaluate vendor support SLAs, documentation quality, and community.\n\n- Trial: most AppExchange solutions offer a trial period — always test in a sandbox before purchasing."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s21_explain_the_use_cases_and_considerations_7", number: 7, question: "The considerations for integrating a third-party field service management system with Service Cloud, including bidirectional sync, case handoff, and scheduling data", answer: "Key considerations:\n\n- Case handoff: when a case requires a field technician, the case details (customer, asset, issue description) must be sent to the field system to create a work order. Map Salesforce case fields to field service work order fields.\n\n- Bidirectional sync: updates in the field service system (work order status, technician notes, parts used) must sync back to Salesforce so the agent has the latest status. Choose a sync mechanism: real-time (webhooks/platform events) vs. batch (scheduled ETL).\n\n- Scheduling data: technician availability and appointment windows may live in the field service system. Surface this data in Salesforce (via External Objects or API) so agents can set customer expectations.\n\n- Data conflict resolution: if both systems can update the same record, define which system is the \"system of record\" to prevent overwrite conflicts.\n\n- Error handling: ensure failed sync messages are logged and retried; silent failures lead to stale data and customer-facing errors.\n\n- Consider Salesforce Field Service (FSL): if the company does not already have a field service system, Salesforce Field Service is the native option that eliminates the integration complexity entirely.")
                    ]
                )
            ]
        )

    ]
}
